>    "state_note": "*** RIDER FROM THE FABLE SEAT 2026-08-27: THE WRITE-ARM BOUND COVERS MORE THAN DATA\nCORRUPTION. 
***\n\nThe CDB dispatch keys on ring OCCUPANCY `== 12` while the write arm is unguarded during\nDATAIN.  So a 
mid-DATAIN write sequence can drive occupancy to exactly 12 and trigger a\n**SPURIOUS COMMAND DISPATCH, which frees 
and replaces `atapi_st`** -- while a transfer is\nlive and holding pointers into it.\n\n*That is a lifetime hazard, 
not a data hazard, and it raises this row above 'the guest\ncorrupts its own transfer'.  Filed from READING; not 
measured.*\n\n*** FILED BY `#473`, AND ITS RATIONALE WAS CORRECTED THE SAME DAY BY TWO SEATS. ***\n\n`#473` bounded 
the two DATAIN arms.  It did NOT bound the primitive: `wdc_addtoinbuf()` has\nno capacity check and `head == tail` 
still means BOTH empty and full.\n\n**THE FILING SAID \"correct and complete for every caller in the tree today\".  
THAT IS\nIMPRECISE, and both the Fable and grok seats named the same live counter-example\nindependently:** the 
data-port WRITE arm pushes guest bytes with no phase guard and no\ncapacity check, and its unexpected-write latch is 
SUPPRESSED precisely because\n`atapi_cmd_in_progress` is set.  A guest writing while `write_in_progress == 0 
&&\natapi_cmd_in_progress == 1` accumulates unboundedly.  **That is the live unbounded route\nand it is independently 
reachable.**\n\n**AND THE REFILL `#473` ADDED IS ITSELF A CAPACITY-UNAWARE CALLER**, as the codex seat put\nit: *\"at 
most 32768 calls is not a capacity bound unless at least 32768 free slots are\nestablished.\"*  On every in-tree 
driver the ring is empty there; on an adversarial\ninterleaved write it is not.\n\n*** PRIORITY ARGUMENT, and it runs 
the OPPOSITE way to the usual one: `#473` made the\nBENIGN route bounded and silent, so the hole now self-announces 
LESS. ***  Before that\nround a legitimate oversized READ(10) would trip the `warned_inbuf_overrun` announce.  
The\nFable seat's reading is that this argues for RAISING the item's priority rather than\ndeferring it -- what was 
lost is a POST-HOC diagnostic that fired after corruption began,\nso danger is unchanged and DISCOVERY LIKELIHOOD 
fell.\n\n**CLOSEABLE.**  `ae68e29` shipped the witness; **`67120fd` (`#495`) shipped the repair** -- the phase\nguard 
now precedes both endian branches and refuses inappropriate ATAPI data-port pushes\n(`dev_wdc.c`, anchor the phase 
guard before the endian split).  **The row's own \"which fix?\"\nquestion is stale.**\n\nCarriers, named: 
`wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` rows **G/H/I** (first, second and third byte after\nre-arm) plus **L** (status after a store 
in PHASE_COMPLETED).  The preserved verbatim-reinstatement\ncontrol **M4** reds all four while the base controls stay 
valid\n(`regress/artifacts/wdcaddtoinbufbound/killtable_20260830/kill_M4.out`).\n\n> **BUT THE CARRIER IS A TWO-LEG, 
UNGATED DETECTOR under [[wiringcliff]]** -- automatic battery\n> coverage is still owed.  *Closing this row does not 
discharge that, and the closure note must say\n> so or it manufactures coverage.*\n\n**One narrower withdrawal inside 
it:** the spurious CDB-dispatch/lifetime rider was already blocked\nat `ae68e29` (dispatch requires `PHASE_CMDOUT`), 
so it must NOT be credited as an additional live\nconsequence of DATA-IN injection.\n\n*Closure PROPOSED, not made; 
ratification is queued for the 2026-09-09\nadjudication.  Evidence: codex closure pass 2026-09-07, 
regress/artifacts/queueunrated/codex_closeable_20260907.txt.*",
