     "opened": "2026-08-30",
     "witness": "harness:MEASURED by running check_probe_wiring.py at --today 2026-09-19 (PASS) and --today 2026-09-21 
(FAIL, 8 problems)",
     "note": "*** EIGHT DATED EXEMPTIONS EXPIRE ON ONE DAY, AND THE CHECKER HARD-FAILS.  MEASURED, NOT REASONED. ***   
   python tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-19   ->  PROBEWIRING_PASS  (46/54 gated, 8 
dated-exempt)\n    python tools/pipeline/check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-21   ->  PROBEWIRING_FAIL  8 
problem(s)\nSo **every commit after 2026-09-20 is blocked** until all eight are wired or the dates are moved.  The 
checker is right to do this -- a dated exemption that never comes due is not an exemption -- but eight coming due 
together is a scheduling hazard rather than a deadline.  *** AND THE POPULATION IS TWICE WHAT THE REGRESS REVIEW 
NAMED. ***  That review found *'FOUR wdc probes share the single-leg-mode debt and the same 2026-09-20 expiry'* and 
recommended ONE design round covering four instruments.  Correct about the wdc cluster; the expiry itself is shared by 
EIGHT probes with FOUR DISTINCT remedies:  **(1) single-leg mode, 4 probes** -- `wdc_altoffset_probe.py`, `_sig_`, 
`_4bit_`, `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py`.  Two-leg differentials; a gate has only one binary.  Two of them additionally 
need a CD-ROM rig (`ciR:`) and one needs a POISONED image the battery does not build.  This is the one-design-round 
cluster.  **(2) `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py`** -- rung 2, ~8 s, no rig image; wants a CONSTRUCTION gate.  Different 
mechanism entirely.  **(3) `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` -> gate 10; `pit8253_latch_probe.py` -> gate 14.**  Each names the 
gate it belongs in; these are wiring, not design.  **(4) `fbpending_drain_probe.py`** -- REPORT-ONLY until 
`EXPECT_CAP` is set, so wiring it without setting that ships a row that cannot fail.  *** THE POINT IS THE SHAPE, NOT 
THE COUNT: four of the eight are CHEAP (two are plain gate wiring, one is a one-variable decision), and only the wdc 
four need a design round. ***  Doing the cheap four first shrinks the cliff from eight to four before it arrives, and 
each is independently shippable.  *Advisory, from the same run: the checker also notes two SELFLABEL discrepancies 
(`pit8253_latch_probe.py` and `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` -- manifest says detector, the probe's own summary line says 
witness).  It flags these as advisory precisely because self-labels are measurably unreliable in both directions.*  
*** ONE OF THE EIGHT WAS ABOUT TO BE GATE-WIRED WITH NO WAY TO DETECT A TRUNCATION.  FIXED PRE-EMPTIVELY 2026-08-30. 
***  `pit8253_latch_probe.py`'s last output was a bare `PASS`/`FAIL` -- a word that occurs in dozens of contexts and 
is not uniquely greppable.  Its only SCREAMING_TOKEN was `print(\"MEASURED\")`, a **mid-run section header** that a 
truncated log still contains.  So a gate row written against it could not have distinguished a completed run from a 
killed one, which is exactly the `killdrivercrash` class arriving in new work rather than old.  Now emits 
`PIT8253_LATCH_DONE`, on both the pass and fail paths -- a completed FAILURE is not a truncation.  *Doing this BEFORE 
the wiring means the gate row can be written correctly the first time.  `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py`, the other 
plain-wiring member, already carries a proper `SH4_BSC_WIDTH_PASS`/`_FAIL` pair and needs nothing.*  *** AND A 
METHODOLOGICAL CORRECTION WORTH MORE THAN THE FIX, because it nearly became a filed defect count. ***  A sweep for 
probes lacking a `_DONE`/`_PASS` token returned **34 of 46**.  That number is MEANINGLESS: gate 10 verifies 
`sh_rounding_probe.py` by grepping `SH_ROUND_RESULT=`, which is a perfectly good completion marker in a different 
shape.  The convention is per-probe and per-gate, so **there is no 34-probe defect and the question is not sweepable** 
-- it is 'does this probe's CONSUMER verify completion, and by what?', one probe at a time.  An earlier version of the 
same sweep put `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` in the missing list, which is how the pattern was caught: it flagged a probe I 
had confirmed compliant ten minutes earlier.  **A pattern that selects the wrong set produces a clean, confident, 
wrong population**, and this is the third instance today.  *** THE CHEAP FOUR, INVESTIGATED READ-ONLY 2026-08-30 SO A 
ROUND CAN START WITHOUT RE-DERIVING ANY OF IT.  Ranked by what each actually needs. ***  **(a) 
`pit8253_latch_probe.py` -> gate 14.**  ALREADY UNBLOCKED: it lacked any completion marker and now emits 
`PIT8253_LATCH_DONE`.  Machine `-E cats`, 10 rows.  Gate 14 is `gate_arm.sh`, which already runs `-E cats`.  Remaining 
work is the exec line plus row checks.  **(b) `sh4_bsc_width_probe.py` -> gate 10.**  Machine `-E landisk`; already 
emits `SH4_BSC_WIDTH_PASS`/`_FAIL`.  **Gate 10 (`gate_sh_rounding.sh`) already execs three probes in exactly the 
needed idiom** -- `python3 <probe>.py \"$PMAX\" \"$KERNEL\" > \"$LOG\" 2>&1 || true`, then `check` rows against the 
log.  Copying that idiom is the whole change.  **(c) `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py` -> a construction gate.**  Rung 2, ~8 s, 
NO RIG IMAGE, takes no positional argv, accepts `--binary PATH` (default `$GXROOT/build/gxemul`), emits 
`HPCMIPS_CTOR_PASS`/`_FAIL`, and already runs its subprocess with a timeout.  `gate_asan_sweep.sh` is the construction 
gate and sweeps 41 machine types.  ONE MISMATCH TO RESOLVE, named rather than discovered later: that gate drives an 
ASAN binary while this probe defaults to `build/gxemul` -- `--binary` makes it parameterisable, so the decision is 
which binary the row should assert against.  **(d) `fbpending_drain_probe.py` -- ONE VARIABLE, BUT NOT A FREE ONE.**  
It is REPORT-ONLY until `--expect-cap N` is set (`EXPECT_CAP = -1` means report only), and **wiring it without 
choosing N ships a row that cannot fail** -- the check-that-cannot-fail shape.  Choosing N needs a measurement, and 
the probe carries a **31.51 s wall-clock floor by construction**, so it is timing-sensitive and must be measured on a 
quiet machine.  Cheapest in code, most expensive in care.  *Ordering follows from that: (a) and (b) are mechanical, 
(c) needs one decision, (d) needs a measurement.  Three of the four can ship before the wdc design round even starts.* 
 *** `8404520` NAMED: that commit shipped this row's FIRST piece of code -- the `PIT8253_LATCH_DONE` sentinel -- and 
the attribution check refused the NEXT commit until the row said so.  Third time today this rule has fired (after 
`census` and `wdcatapistswap`), and every one was a real gap rather than a nuisance. ***  The row stays OPEN: one of 
eight probes is unblocked, seven remain, and the cliff is unmoved until they are wired.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 
2026-09-09, phase assess -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  RE-DERIVED 
FROM THE CHECKER, and three of this project's own records about this row were wrong.  (1) The exemption set is what 
`grep -n 2026-09-20 check_probe_wiring.py' returns, and the checker states the same count in its own comment -- the 
ledger TITLE said eight.  (2) The comparison is `d < today', so the block is valid ON its stamped date and first reds 
the NEXT day; CHECKPOINT.md said it fails on the stamped date.  (3) Of the six, only fbpending_drain and hpcmips_ctor 
have a measurement-driven nonzero exit; the four WDC harnesses end in a bare `main()' or fall off the end after 
writing a table file.  CHECKPOINT.md said three.  *** SO WIRING THE FOUR AS THEY STAND WOULD SHIP FOUR GATE SECTIONS 
THAT CANNOT FAIL -- this project's own named class, four at once, installed BY the deadline. ***  AND A DERIVER OF 
MINE MEASURED A CORRELATE while correcting someone else's count: `grep -cE 'exit\\([1-9]'' scored three of the four as 
failable, matching two `os._exit(127)' calls in forked pty CHILDREN (which give the PARENT no failure status) and one 
COMMENT quoting a removed call.  The class is 'does the parent exit nonzero when a row fails'; the regex tested the 
TEXT.  Honest deriver: `sys\\.exit\\(' plus reading how each file ENDS.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase assess 
-- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  *** THE DISPOSITION IS AN OWNER 
DECISION AND IS DELIBERATELY NOT TAKEN. ***  Every one of the six reason strings names a prerequisite that does not 
exist TODAY and is still true: an unset EXPECT_CAP ('wiring is a design step first'), a construction-gate home, a 
single-leg mode for the two-leg differentials, a ciR: CD-ROM rig and a poisoned CD image 'the battery does not build'. 
 So the 2026-09-09 adjudication's 'four cheap exemptions as a small side-commit regardless of ranking' IS NOT 
EXECUTABLE -- it inherited the wrong title.  The useful split is not cheap/expensive but CONTROLLABLE (fbpending, 
hpcmips -- blocked on decisions we make, and both already exit nonzero) vs INFRASTRUCTURE-BLOCKED (the four WDC, 
needing an instrument mode AND rig images, and none of them failable).  Options: (a) re-date carrying each blocker's 
evidence, or (b) six design rounds.  If (a), each entry gains the owing round's id -- the checker's own standard is 
'an EXEMPTION naming the round that owes it.  Debt that expires, not debt that disappears', and a bare re-date 
converts (a) into debt that disappears.",
