as ONE block on 2026-09-28 -- `exemptcliff` owns that; FILED from the round: hpcmipsc2, exemptcal, m5dayb. 
***\n\n\n\n**RESIDUAL-REMAINS.**  `8404520` added PIT's completion sentinel, and PIT plus SH4 BSC were\nsubsequently 
wired.  **Six exemptions remain, all dated 2026-09-20, and the checker rejects them\nfrom the 21st**: 
`fbpending_drain_probe.py`, `hpcmips_ctor_probe.py`, the three `wdc_altoffset*`\nprobes, and `wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py` 
-- *which is the carrier for [[wdcaddtoinbufbound]]'s proposed\nclosure, so the two rows are coupled.*\n\n*** THE ROW 
IS FRAMED AS \"WIRE SIX PROBES\" AND THAT IS THE WRONG JOB.  FOUR OF THE SIX CANNOT SERVE\nAS GATE ROWS AT ALL, AND 
WIRING THEM ON THE DEADLINE WOULD SHIP FOUR GATE SECTIONS THAT CANNOT FAIL.\nFound by a codex ANALYSIS job 2026-09-08, 
VERIFIED here. ***\n\nThe cliff is real and was reproduced first: `check_probe_wiring.py --today 2026-09-21` 
gives\n`PROBEWIRING_FAIL  6 problem(s)`, and `--today 2026-09-19` passes.  The comparison is `d < today`,\nso the 
exemptions are good THROUGH the 20th and rejected from the 21st.\n\n**codex's finding, which the brief did not ask 
for:** *\"the signature and 4-bit probes print\nmeasurements without enforcing a pass/fail result ... All four WDC 
harnesses can finish with exit\nstatus zero after reporting failures.\"*\n\n**MEASURED here, per probe -- count of 
nonzero-exit paths and of PASS/FAIL tokens:**\n\n    fbpending_drain_probe.py       nonzero-exit 2   tokens PASS/FAIL  
    ENFORCEABLE\n    hpcmips_ctor_probe.py          nonzero-exit 3   tokens PASS/FAIL/_RESULT=   ENFORCEABLE\n    
wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py        nonzero-exit 0   token  FAIL only      *** CANNOT FAIL ***\n    wdc_altoffset_probe.py  
       nonzero-exit 0   token  FAIL only      *** CANNOT FAIL ***\n    wdc_altoffset_4bit_probe.py    nonzero-exit 1   
NO pass/fail token    partial\n    wdc_altoffset_sig_probe.py     nonzero-exit 0   NO pass/fail token    *** CANNOT 
FAIL ***\n\n> **THREE OF THE SIX HAVE NO NONZERO-EXIT PATH WHATEVER, AND TWO OF THOSE EMIT NO RESULT TOKEN\n> EITHER.  
A gate section calling them greps for nothing and tests an exit status that is always 0 --\n> the 
CHECK-THAT-CANNOT-FAIL class, in its purest form.**\n\n*** AND THE DEADLINE IS WHAT MAKES THIS DANGEROUS RATHER THAN 
MERELY UNTIDY. ***  On 2026-09-20 the\nchecker starts blocking commits, and the obvious way to unblock it is to add 
six gate invocations.\n**Four of those would be inert, and the checker would then go GREEN -- it verifies that a gate 
CALLS\nthe probe, not that the call can fail.**  The cliff would have converted a loud, honest red into a\nquiet, 
permanent false green, and nothing downstream would ever say so.\n\n*** THE DISPOSITION QUESTION IS THE MANIFEST 
CLASS, NOT THE WIRING, AND THE CHECKER ITSELF ALREADY\nSAYS SO. ***  Its own output carries:\n\n    note  SELFLABEL  
wdc_addtoinbuf_probe.py: manifest says detector, its own summary line says\n                     witness.  Advisory 
only -- self-labels are measurably unreliable in both\n                     directions.\n\n**A WITNESS reproduces a 
defect on a pre-fix build and is done; a DETECTOR must run forever and must\nbe able to go red.**  Four of these six 
behave like witnesses or measurement harnesses, and the\nmanifest classes all six as detectors.  So the candidate 
dispositions are:\n\n  1. **wire the two that enforce** (`fbpending_drain`, `hpcmips_ctor`) -- cheap, and codex 
names\n     plausible homes: `gate_arm.sh` already supplies the CATS stub for the first,\n     `gate_asan_sweep.sh` 
covers machine construction for the second;\n  2. for the four, EITHER give each a real verdict line and a nonzero 
exit -- which makes them\n     detectors in fact rather than by declaration -- OR reclassify them in the manifest, 
which is a\n     records change with no gate cost;\n  3. NOT: extend the dates.  That is the option the cliff exists 
to prevent.\n\n**Two corrections to the brief's own premises, both from codex and both checked:** `gate_wdc.sh`\nDOES 
NOT EXIST (I assumed it might), and `run.sh` runs **16** gates, none of which invokes any of the\nsix -- 
`gate_hygiene`'s references to them are source-TEXT inspections, not invocations.\n\n*** WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, said 
