(regress/artifacts/lcabadval/codex_scoping_20260907.txt) while scoping an unrelated device, and\nverified at both 
ends.\n\n    memory_alpha.c   (*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift;\n    memory_rw.c      offset 
= paddr & offset_mask;\n\nThe walk returns a PAGE-BASE address, so `paddr & offset_mask' is ZERO and every access 
within a page\nresolves to that page's first bytes.\n\n*** THE HEDGE IS THE SEAT'S OWN AND IT IS CORRECT: the 
DIRECT-MAPPED paths avoid it. ***  Both early returns\n-- K0SEG (`vaddr & 0x000003ffffffffffULL') and the `ptbr == 0' 
bootstrap hack -- MASK the vaddr and therefore\nKEEP the offset bits.  Only the real walk drops them, and it is 
reached in user mode, or outside K0SEG once\nptbr != 0.\n\n**REACHABILITY IS THE OPEN QUESTION AND IT BOUNDS THE 
SEVERITY BOTH WAYS.**  memory_alpha.c carries seven\nTODOs and an explicit fatal(\"Alpha kernel virtual memory seems 
to want to be used. Not yet implemented\") +\nexit(1), so Alpha VM here is immature and a bootable configuration may 
never reach the walk -- in which case\nthis is latent rather than live.  **HIGH is PROVISIONAL on that**: if a guest 
reaches it the effect is total\nmemory-access corruption; if none can, the row is documented-only.  A round takes the 
reachability question\nFIRST; the fix itself is adding the offset back.\n\nThe function is live, not dead code: 
cpu_alpha.c assigns `cpu->translate_v2p = alpha_translate_v2p' and\ncpu_dyntrans.c calls it.\n\n*** REACHABILITY PASS, 
codex seat 2026-09-07 (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_reachability_20260907.txt).  THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED 
BY\nEXHAUSTIVE ENUMERATION -- AND MY OWN SUMMARY OF THE IMPACT WAS TOO BROAD.  Corrected here. ***\n\n**CONFIRMED, and 
by walking the values rather than arguing:** *\"I enumerated all 8,192 offsets: the walk\nexpression produces offset 
zero throughout, differing from the offset-preserving address for 8,191 offsets.\nThe masking paths preserve offsets; 
exhaustive enumeration also confirmed preservation throughout all three\nbootstrap alias regions.\"*  So the defect is 
real, the direct-mapped arms are genuinely safe, and there are\nTHREE bootstrap alias regions rather than the two I 
had read.\n\n*** MY OVERCLAIM, CORRECTED: this row said `every walked access would land at its page base'.  THAT IS 
TOO\nBROAD. ***  codex: *\"Slow-path accesses consume the incorrect physical offset, but cached Alpha 
loads/stores\ncalculate `addr & 8191' themselves.  Subsequent cache hits can therefore access the correct 
within-page\nbytes.\"*  So the corruption is confined to the SLOW PATH; once a page is cached, the load/store 
instructions\nrecompute the offset and read the right bytes.  **That materially narrows the blast radius** and it is 
the\nsecond time tonight this seat has narrowed one of my summaries -- the first being the class-scan 
denominator.\n\n**REACHABILITY -- the answer is NOT `latent', and it is not `live' either.**  codex found *\"no source 
barrier\nmaking a guest-instruction witness impossible on the existing description\"*, and named the smallest 
decisive\nobservation: one COMPLETED guest data access on the existing `eb164' machine that reaches the walk with 
a\nnonzero page offset and whose caller completes the access on the returned page-base address.  **A PTBR\nwatchpoint 
or a walker-entry breakpoint is explicitly NOT enough** -- reaching the walk is not the same as an\naccess completing 
wrongly.\n\n**What it could not settle, and said so plainly:** *\"I cannot name a demonstrated working Alpha OS boot 
from\nthe allowed files.\"*  So the honest status is **WITNESSABLE IN PRINCIPLE, UNWITNESSED IN FACT** -- which is\na 
different thing from latent, and it is what a round takes first.\n\n**HIGH stands but its warrant changes**: not 
`every access is corrupted' (false), but `the slow path is\ncorrupted for 8,191 of 8,192 offsets, on a path with no 
established guest that reaches it'.\n\n*** THE BOOT QUESTION IS ANSWERED, AND IT REVERSES MY CONCERN: ALL FOUR ALPHA 
ROWS ARE COLD-DEBUGGER\nREACHABLE.  NO OS BOOT IS REQUIRED.  codex, 2026-09-07 
(regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt). ***\n\nI had flagged the risk that the Alpha queue was 
accumulating well-analysed rows on a machine nothing can\nexercise.  It is not: *\"EB164 or AlphaServer 4100 provides 
the required unmodified machine description.\nThere is no architectural requirement to boot an OS first.\"*  Debugger 
word writes go through the memory\nhelpers, Alpha fetches and decodes through its actual memory path, and the shared 
breakpoint machinery\napplies -- which is exactly the rung-3 cold-debugger shape this project's ladder admits.\n\nIts 
classification of all four:\n\n    page-walk drops the offset      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   needs page-table state; 
the bootstrap\n                                                              identity mapping bypasses the walk\n    
PALcode register mismatch       COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   swpctx is reached by guest dispatch\n    unsupported 
fault-delivery      COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   failed access returns without an exception\n    guest-reachable process 
exit    COLD-DEBUGGER REACHABLE   the bootstrap translator's special branch\n\n**The qualification on THIS row is the 
one that matters for its witness:** the walk needs page-table state,\nand *the ordinary bootstrap identity mapping 
bypasses the walk*.  So a probe must first establish a non-zero\n`apcb_ptbr' and a valid three-level table -- which is 
real work, and is why this row is still the hardest of\nthe five despite being the most severe if 
reached.\n\n**[[alphaswpctxptbr]] is a confound for that witness and must be fixed first:** it clobbers `apcb_ptbr` 
with\nthe flags word, so any probe that sets up a context via `swpctx` and then observes a bad translation cannot\nsay 
which defect produced it.\n\n*** THE CONFOUND IS NOW MEASURED, NOT SUSPECTED.  2026-09-07. ***\n[[alphaswpctxptbr]] 
has a rung-3 witness as of today: on the committed build, `PCB_A+16` reads the\nFLAGS word (`0xbbbb2222`) where the 
ptbr (`0xaaaa1111`) is required -- `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`,\nHEAD 7/9, artifacts under 
`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/`.\n\nSo the ordering this row already asserted is now backed by a measurement: **a 
witness for the\npage-walk offset defect must either fix the swap first or establish `apcb_ptbr` by a route that 
does\nnot go through `swpctx`** -- otherwise a bad translation cannot be attributed to the walk.  The\nharness's own 
bootstrap shows such a route exists (debugger `put d` writes the PCB directly while\n`ptbr == 0`), which is the more 
promising of the two.\n\n*** GROK WITNESS DESIGN 2026-09-07 
(`regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/grok_witness_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: BUILDABLE on the committed tree, and it 
**does not wait on [[alphaswpctxptbr]]** -- which\nrefutes the premise this brief was written around. ***\n\n**S4 WAS 
TOO STRONG.**  `swpctx` IS the only writer of `apcb_ptbr` (verified: the debugger exports\nonly `pc` and the GPRs; 
`wrvptptr` writes the unused `vptptr`; `machine_alpha.c` puts the L1 PFN in\n**`a1`**, not the PCB).  **But the swap 
is only a confound if `ptbr` and `flags` DIFFER.**\n\n> **PUT THE SAME L1 PFN IN BOTH PCB FIELDS.**  Then the 
defective line and the corrected line both\n> leave `apcb_ptbr = L1_PFN`, and the walk is armed identically either 
way.  One `call_pal 0x30`,\n> and the confound is gone without fixing anything.\n\n*And the inverse is a trap: putting 
the PFN only in `flags` with `ptbr = 0` arms the walk on HEAD and\nleaves IDENTITY MAPPING on the fixed PAL -- so 
post-fix green would be the bootstrap, not the repair.*\n\n**S3 IS SHARPER THAN I WROTE, AND IT KILLS MY Q3.**  The 
fast path caches the page from\n`paddr & ~offset_mask`, so the cached host page is the **correct physical page** and a 
later hit at\nANY in-page offset reads the right bytes.  **The walked paddr is wrong only for the FILL.**  So *what\nN 
avoids the cached path* is the wrong question -- **N does not select the slow path.**  The design is\n`N = 8`, cold 
cache, **ONE shot, and do not retry the load: the second access is a hit and goes green\non HEAD.**  (An unaligned N 
does force the generic path, but that is an emulator gap rather than\nAlpha, and a later alignment check would kill 
the witness for the wrong reason.)\n\n*** THE PLANTED-PAST-RAM EQUIVALENT FOR THIS PROBE IS NOT AN ADDRESS BOUND -- IT 
IS PFN-vs-BYTE. ***\n`apcb_ptbr = 0x01050000` gives `base = 0x01050000 << 13`, past RAM, `memory_paddr_to_hostaddr` 
NULL,\n`not_found`.  **The PCB and the PTE high halves hold PFNs, not byte addresses.**\n\n**THE TABLE, concrete and 
off both the PROM pocket (`0x10000`/`0x10100`/`0x12000`/`0x14000`) and the\nsibling probes' scratch:**\n\n    L1   
0x01050000  PFN 0x828      L1[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x829) | VALID\n    L2   0x01052000  PFN 0x829      L2[0] = 
PTE_FROM_PFN(0x82A) | VALID\n    L3   0x01054000  PFN 0x82A      L3[0] = PTE_FROM_PFN(0x82B) | VALID\n    data 
0x01056000  PFN 0x82B      apcb_ptbr = 0x828   <- the PFN\n\nTest vaddr in `[0, 8192)` so `i1 = i2 = i3 = 0`.  **Only 
`ALPHA_PTE_VALID` is load-bearing** -- the\nwalk never tests KR/KW/UR/UW, FAULT_ON_*, ASM or granularity.  *\"Do not 
set bits the walk does not\nimplement and then call them required\"* -- the addition licence, applied to a 
probe.\n\n**THE THREE-WAY ORACLE, which is better than the two-way I proposed, and rests on a fact I had\nwrong.**  My 
Q4 said a failed walk \"faults or returns zero\".  **False for the guest-visible\nregister:** `LS_GENERIC_N` returns 
on `memory_rw` failure **without writing the destination**.  So\npre-set the destination to a third sentinel `S`:\n\n  
  S unchanged  the walk never completed\n    0            the walk succeeded onto UNMAPPED physical (memblock NULL 
still returns ok)\n    A            the walk hit the right page at offset 0   <- THE DEFECT\n    B            
correct\n\n**THE POLARITY TRAP, and it is the `lcaconfwidth` inversion wearing different clothes:** plant `B`\nTHROUGH 
the walk (a `put d` or `stq` at `vaddr+N` once `ptbr` is live) and on HEAD that store lands\nat `data+0`; the later 
load of `vaddr+N` also lands at `data+0` and reads `B`.  **HEAD goes green,\nmeasuring the defect against itself.**  A 
cousin: planting at vaddr `0`/`N` while `ptbr == 0` hits\nPHYSICAL `0`/`N`, not the data page, and both polarities 
collapse.  **Plant `A` and `B` only via\nidentity-physical or K0SEG of the DATA PAGE, before the walk is 
used.**\n\n**CONTROLS:** `data0 k0` and `dataN k0` (K0SEG reads of the same physical page -- they do not fill 
the\ntest vaddr's translation slot, different key), `walk mapped` (dest not 0 and not S), and the ABSENCE\nof 
`alpha_translate_v2p: ... wasn't found`.  *`A` on the walked load with those green is itself the\nproof the walk ran 
-- a stronger channel than a same-decode round-trip, because the defect signature\nnames the page.*\n\n**READ beats 
WRITE as the witness** (one `ldq`, three-way register, single `want`).  A walked `stq`\nis the same mechanism and 
needs the same three table pages -- *the write form does not save a PTE* --\nand its read-back must go through K0SEG, 
since a walked read-back is a cache hit that hides HEAD.\n\n**Four smaller facts, each load-bearing:** code must stay 
at `k0(P_CODE)` after `ptbr` is live (a\nphysical-looking PC walks with `i2=32` and a 3-PTE table does not map it); 
stay in kernel mode, since\nuser mode makes K0SEG I-fetch walk; `pte2`/`pte3` invalid additionally `fatal(\"TODO: 
...\")`, useful as\na setup channel; and `store_64bit_word` sign-extends addresses whose bits 63:32 are clear, so 
physical\n`put d` stays below 2 GB or uses K0SEG.\n\n*** WITNESS BUILT AND RUN THE SAME HOUR THE DESIGN LANDED.  HEAD 
5/6 -- THE OFFSET DROP IS NOW\nMEASURED, not derived. ***\n\n    data0 k0     0xaaaa0000aaaa   0xaaaa0000aaaa   ok    
CONTROL: A really is at data+0\n    dataN k0     0xbbbb0000bbbb   0xbbbb0000bbbb   ok    CONTROL: B really is at 
data+N\n    walk found   absent           absent           ok    CONTROL: walked to a valid L3\n    walk stored  
0xaaaa0000aaaa   NOT 0x55000055   ok    CONTROL: the load DID write the register\n    walk mapped  0xaaaa0000aaaa   
NOT 0x0          ok    CONTROL: not unmapped physical\n    walk off     0xaaaa0000aaaa   0xbbbb0000bbbb   FAIL  the 
walked load returned data+0\n\n**A real guest `ldq` of `vaddr+8`, through a hand-built three-level page table, on an 
unmodified\nmachine description, returned the value at the page BASE.**  The three-way sentinel earned its place:\nthe 
register was written (so the walk completed), the result was not 0 (so it did not land on\nunmapped physical), and it 
was `A` (so the walk found the right PAGE and dropped the OFFSET).  Those\nthree are separable only because the 
sentinel exists -- a two-way oracle would have reported the same\nred for all three causes.\n\n**EVERY ONE OF GROK'S 
DESIGN CORRECTIONS WAS LOAD-BEARING AND ALL OF THEM HELD IN PRACTICE:**\nthe same-PFN-in-both-PCB-fields trick 
defeated the [[alphaswpctxptbr]] confound without fixing it,\nso this row did NOT wait on that repair; the 
PFN-not-byte-address point is what keeps `base` inside\nRAM; K0SEG planting kept the setup off the walk (planting `B` 
through the walk would have made HEAD\ngreen against itself); and the single cold shot mattered -- a retry is a cache 
hit that reads the\ncorrect bytes and greens on HEAD.\n\n*Two defects of my own, caught before the run: dead 
placeholder code, and a 40-bit sentinel handed to\na `load_const` that builds 32-bit constants -- it would have 
truncated silently and the sentinel row\nwould have compared the wrong value.  The row now pins a value `load_const` 
can actually build.*\n\n*** GROK FIX DESIGN 2026-09-07.  VERDICT: RATIFY THE ASSIGNMENT, NOT THE ROUND -- the 
same\nruling as `#507`, and it REFUTED MY OWN STATED WORRY while finding a worse one. ***\n\n**THE FIX:**\n\n    
(*return_paddr) = (ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift)\n        | (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1));\n\n`|` 
and `+` are numerically identical here (the shifted PFN has bits 12:0 clear), but they are\ndifferent SENTENCES: **`|` 
is every other walk in this tree** -- ARM, m88k, MIPS, PPC, SH -- while\n`+` is this function's fixed bootstrap 
windows.  **And derive the mask from `pageSizeShift`, not a\nliteral**: `:88`'s `0x1fff` is correct for a fixed 8 KB 
PROM pocket, and hardcoding it beside\n`<< pageSizeShift` is how a mask and its shift drift apart.\n\n*The parentheses 
are load-bearing: `1 << pageSizeShift - 1` is `0x1000`, not `0x1fff`.  **The\nexisting `N = 8` row DOES kill that 
one** (`8 & 0x1000 == 0` -> VAL_A).*\n\n*** MY `0xfff`/`0x3fff` PAIRING WAS FALSE, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS. ***\nI 
wrote that a mask of `0xfff` or `0x3fff` would be *\"correct at N = 8 and wrong further into the\npage\"*.  **`0x3fff` 
is not wrong anywhere in the page:** the extra bit is bit 13, and bit 13 of an\noffset is zero for every byte of an 8 
KB page.  `+0x1008` still passes under it.  **`0xfff` is the\ndangerous one** -- it is the 4 KB copy 
(`MRW_OFFSET_MASK` on every non-Alpha build,\n`DYNTRANS_PAGESIZE` 4096), and it leaves **the upper 4 KB of every 
walked page still broken**.\n\n**SIX LESSER EDITS ALSO SCORE 6/6 on the committed probe** (`VADDR = 0`, `N = 8`): 
masks `0xfff`,\n`0x3fff`, `0x7fff` (a copy of `:81` rather than `:88`), any mask including bit 3, **unmasked\n`+ 
vaddr`** -- correct only because the VPN is 0 at page 0 -- and a hardcoded `| 8`.  **A second cold\nrow is owed**, at 
a vaddr whose VPN is non-zero and an offset in the upper 4 KB.\n\n**THREE CALLERS ENUMERATED, and one is a THIRD 
consequence I did not know:**\n\n    memory_rw.c MEMORY_RW_ONE_PAGE   needs the FULL address     <- the defect\n    
cpu_dyntrans.c I-fetch miss      tolerates either; strips the offset itself\n    debugger_cmds.c watchpoint       
needs the FULL address     <- A WALKED WATCHPOINT TODAY\n                                                              
     LANDS AT THE PAGE BASE\n\n**No caller does `paddr + (vaddr & mask)`, so the mirror defect is absent and a 
post-fix 6/6 is not\nhiding a double-add.**  Q5 confirmed independently: the fill strips before 
caching\n(`memory_paddr_to_hostaddr(mem, paddr & ~offset_mask, ...)`), `update_translation_table` asserts\npage 
alignment, and the fast path uses the VIRTUAL offset -- so the walked offset is consumed exactly\nonce.\n\n**KEEP THE 
`// TODO.`**  Restoring the offset leaves it true: `flags` is never read\n(`FLAG_WRITEFLAG`, `FLAG_INSTR`, 
`FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` all ignored), `ALPHA_PTE_KR/KW/UR/UW` and\n`FAULT_ON_*` are untested, granularity hints are 
ignored, and **`return 2` means WRITABLE -- so\n`memory_rw` caches every walked Alpha page as writable.**  *Deleting 
the comment would record the\ntranslator as finished.  It is not.*\n\n**THREE MORE, FILED NOT FIXED:** `:81` is the 
WRONG neighbour to copy (`0x7fff`, a 32 KB mask -- a\nseat told to *\"copy the in-function idiom\"* can copy the wrong 
one, and the current witness cannot\nsee it); `flags` being dead means a failed walk still prints `wasn't found` even 
under\n`FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS`, which is exactly the debugger-watchpoint path; and **the function uses TWO NAMES\nfor 13** 
-- `ALPHA_PAGESHIFT` on the L1/L2 physical addresses, local `pageSizeShift` on the indices\nand the final shift -- so 
the new mask must couple to `pageSizeShift` or the two can diverge on one\nline.\n\n*** `#508` MEASURED: 5/7 -> 7/7, 
SAME SCRIPT.  The round is complete and held for the 9th. ***\n\n    walk off   0xaaaa0000aaaa -> 0xbbbb0000bbbb\n    
walk vpn   0xcccc0000cccc -> 0xdddd0000dddd     <- the row the fix review demanded\n\nFive controls green on both 
runs.  All four trees propagated; three rebuilt with the two-part\nclearance CLEAN/FRESH; `est/` part 2 N/A.  
**Nothing committed** -- the Fable hold stands.\n\n*The `walk vpn` row is the third instance today of a fix review 
ratifying an edit and refusing the\nROUND because the witness could not distinguish the fix from a lesser one.  In all 
three cases the\nmissing row cost one readback and caught something the author had not considered.*\n\n*** COMMIT 
ATTRIBUTION RESTORED 2026-09-08: this row's fix shipped in **`7a41b3f`** and the\n`commits` field was empty.  Verified 
before writing, not assumed. ***\n\n    git show --stat 7a41b3f        touches src/cpus/memory_alpha.c\n    git show 
7a41b3f -- src/cpus/memory_alpha.c\n                                   contains the `#508' round tag 4 times\n\n**ALL 
FOUR ROUNDS IN THAT COMMIT HAD THE SAME GAP.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508:\nfour test-first rounds, 
three new rung-3 witnesses, and a witness row that could not fail\"*, and\n**not one of the four rows carried its 
commit.**  `sgirergbroundtrip` (#505) was found first, by\naccident, while scoping an unrelated round in the same 
file; the other three were found by asking\nthe general question afterwards.\n\n> **THE CLASS: A COMMIT THAT SHIPS 
SEVERAL ROUNDS LOSES THE PER-ROW ATTRIBUTION FOR ALL OF THEM.**\n> The committer is performing ONE act and the ledger 
records N rows, so the per-row field is the\n> thing that falls out.  A one-round commit does not have this failure 
mode -- which is why it has\n> not been seen before and why it appeared four times at once.\n\n*The `state` field is 
deliberately NOT touched here.  `in work` may be correct for a row with\nresidual obligations, and changing it would 
be a claim I have not checked.  Only the attribution,\nwhich is unambiguous, is filled in.*  #508.  The page-table 
walk returned `PFN << pageSizeShift' (the PAGE BASE) and dropped the in-page offset; HEAD now ORs `vaddr & ((1ULL << 
pageSizeShift) - 1)' back in -- VERIFIED LIVE AT HEAD.  Two-direction witness with a second row (`walk vpn') that 
exists because the fix review REFUSED a 6/6 which six lesser masks also scored.  NO KILL TABLE: the detector IS the 
witness (check_probe_wiring classifies it CLASS_WITNESS) and NO GATE RUNS IT -- that is `wiringcliff` debt on its own 
row, the same footing as the other closed Alpha witnesses, not a hold on this one. CLOSED 2026-09-17 on the grok 
