UNDER-COUNTED 9 AS 6**, because the pattern I checked required a severity token `(LOW|MED|HIGH)` and three headings 
carry only `(closed)`.  *Sixth correlate-keyed check of this session, committed inside the audit of the fifth.* 
Codex's real verdict, recorded now: **CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8; KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9.**  *** L4 -- MY 'SPLIT BY SEAT CLASS' 
ADJUDICATION IS A WRONG RECORD, AND THE FABLE SEAT SHOWED IT IS FALSE ON THE PACKET'S OWN DATA.  Corrected 2026-09-17. 
***  I wrote that the four FILE-READING seats reached CLOSE 1-8 / KEEP-OPEN 9 while the PACKET-FED seats kept rows 
open. **Codex is file-reading and its verdict line reads `CLOSE 1, 2, 4, 6, 7, 8.  KEEP-OPEN 3, 5, 9`** -- I tabulated 
it as CLOSE 1-8.  Meanwhile minimax (packet-fed) CLOSED rows 1 and 5, kimi-sub CLOSED 1/3/4/6/7, glm CLOSED 
1/3/4/6/7/8.  **The classes do not sort the verdicts.** *** AND THE RULING WAS CIRCULAR, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH 
KEEPING. ***  My own brief's S0 told the seats *\"an unsettleable row stays open, which is the safe direction\"* -- 
which converts every honest not-checkable into a KEEP-OPEN vote -- and I then read those votes back as evidence that 
the doctrine was working.  **I instructed the behaviour and then cited it as a finding.** **THE RULING FORM THAT 
SHOULD HAVE BEEN WRITTEN** sorts the packet-fed KEEP-OPENs BY KIND, not by class: (a) NOT-CHECKABLE-FROM-PACKET, 
*discharged by naming the file* -- row 2's condition (c), row 8's figures, deepseek's rows 1/3/4/6/7 -- where *\"the 
file-reading seats verified it\" is the right answer ONLY when the file is named*; and (b) SUBSTANTIVE, adjudicated on 
merit regardless of class -- row 9 (all four right), row 5's arithmetic, row 5's unnamed labels (raised by kimi-sub 
and glm, the SAME finding as codex and opus5, so a class split puts those seats on the wrong side of their own 
finding). *** AND ONE SUBSTANTIVE PACKET-FED CLAIM WAS FALSE AND A CLASS RULING IS EXACTLY HOW IT GOES UNREFUTED: *** 
glm's *\"nothing pins the suppression side, 0x08 must NOT fire\"* is answered by the row `a routine 0x08 preserves the 
command` in `diff_wdc_identify.c`.  **That deserved a refutation by file, and my ruling gave it a category instead.** 
**THE COST OF THE MIS-STATEMENT:** it hid codex's row-3 dissent (the row now re-opened on a measurement), and it 
converted a three-seat finding on row 5 into \"the file-reading seats said CLOSE\".",
     "entries": [
      {
       "seat": "grok",
       "phase": "review",
       "date": "2026-09-17",
       "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "opened": "2026-09-07",
     "title": "cpu_alpha_palcode.c swpctx: apcb_ptbr is assigned TWICE -- the second write puts new_flags into the 
PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER, and apcb_flags is never assigned at all",
     "witness": "probe:regress/alpha_harness_probe.py -- HEAD 7/10 -> fix 10/10 
(artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/probe_head10_20260907.out / probe_fix_20260907.out)",
>    "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-07.  **RAISED BY THE CODEX SEAT** under the found-something-else licence, on a brief 
that asked\nonly whether Alpha machines can run anything (regress/artifacts/alphapfnoffset/codex_boot_20260907.txt) -- 
and VERIFIED by reading before\nfiling.\n\n`PAL_OSF1_swpctx' loads a new context and assigns it into the pcb.  
**`apcb_ptbr' is assigned TWICE and\n`apcb_flags' is never assigned at all:**\n\n    // Update all variables in the 
pcb simultaneously:\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ksp    = new_ksp;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_usp    = new_usp;\n    
cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_ptbr;      <- correct\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_cpc    = new_cpc;\n    
cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_asn    = new_asn;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_unique = new_unique;\n    
cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr   = new_flags;     <- *** should be apcb_flags ***\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv0 = 
new_decrsv0;\n    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_decrsv1 = new_decrsv1;\n\n*** WHY THIS OUTRANKS THE OTHER ALPHA ROWS: 
`apcb_ptbr' IS THE PAGE TABLE BASE REGISTER. ***\n`alpha_translate_v2p()' opens with `uint64_t base = 
cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr << ALPHA_PAGESHIFT;' -- so\nafter ANY `swpctx' the page-table walk reads its tables from 
an address derived from the FLAGS word.  Every\ntranslation after a context switch walks garbage.\n\n**And 
`apcb_flags' keeps the OUTGOING context's value**, because nothing ever writes it -- a second, quieter\ndefect from 
the same line.\n\n**THE COMMENT DIRECTLY ABOVE ASSERTS WHAT THE CODE DOES NOT DO:** *\"Update all variables in the 
pcb\nsimultaneously\"*.  Two of the nine are wrong -- one clobbered, one never set.  That is the second time in\nthis 
one function that a comment states an intent the code contradicts; the other is [[alphaswpctxreg]].\n\n**FIX: one 
token** -- the second `apcb_ptbr' becomes `apcb_flags'.  As with its sibling, the intent is not in\ndoubt: `new_flags' 
is loaded from the context block and would otherwise be dead, and `apcb_flags' exists in\nthe struct and would 
otherwise never be written.\n\n**REACHABILITY: cold-debugger reachable, per the same pass** -- `swpctx' is an active 
handler reached by\nordinary guest instruction dispatch with no OS dependency.  A witness plants a `call_pal 0x30' and 
observes\n`apcb_ptbr' afterwards.\n\n*** THE INTERACTION WITH [[alphapfnoffset]] IS WORTH STATING, because it changes 
how that round should be\ndesigned: this defect CORRUPTS THE WALK'S BASE, and that one CORRUPTS THE WALK'S RESULT. *** 
 A witness for\neither must control for the other -- a probe that does a `swpctx' and then observes a bad translation 
cannot\nattribute it without pinning which of the two produced it.  **Fix this one first**: it is certain, one 
token,\nand it removes a confound from the harder row.\n\n*** DESIGN PASS, codex 2026-09-07 
(regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_design_20260907.txt).  THE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED AND TWO OF MY\nSUPPORTING 
PREMISES WERE WRONG.  Corrected here. ***\n\nVerdict: *\"A and B are defects, and both proposed corrections are right. 
 They are TWO changes under your\nreopening rule.  `apcb_flags is never written' is false; no third destination typo 
was found.\"*\n\n**CORRECTION 1 -- `apcb_flags' IS WRITTEN, and I filed the opposite as fact.**  This row said it 
*\"is never\nassigned at all\"*.  `grep -rn apcb_flags src/' returns `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:221' -- **`wrfen' assigns 
it**\nfrom `r[ALPHA_A0]' and then writes it back to PCB memory, and `swpctx' itself READS the field when saving 
the\noutgoing context.\n\n**The honest statement is narrower and still a defect:** `swpctx' never assigns `apcb_flags' 
WHEN LOADING A\nNEW CONTEXT, so the incoming context's flags are silently dropped and the outgoing context's value 
persists\nacross the switch.  That is a real defect; *never written anywhere* was not.\n\n**CORRECTION 2 -- 
`new_flags' is NOT dead, and the distinction matters.**  I wrote that it *\"would otherwise\nbe dead\"*.  codex: it 
*\"has exactly one use after its declaration: the erroneous PTBR assignment.  It is\notherwise unused, but **not dead 
in the current program**: it changes PTBR.\"*  My phrasing implied the\ncompiler would flag it; it would not, because 
the erroneous line consumes it.\n\n**A QUALIFICATION ON THE SIBLING [[alphaswpctxreg]] THAT BOUNDS ITS IMPACT:** the 
handler captures its input\ninto a LOCAL `a0' before dispatch, so overwriting `r[ALPHA_A0]' does **not** change which 
context is loaded.\nThat defect is therefore purely a wrong RETURN VALUE -- it does not corrupt the switch itself.  I 
had not\nestablished that, and it is the difference between a reporting bug and a state-corruption bug.\n\n**SCOPE: 
TWO CHANGES, NOT ONE.**  I intended to fix both in one diff -- same function, same class.  codex\nrules they are two 
under the reopening rule.  **Accepted**: the observables differ (a guest register versus\ninternal PCB state reachable 
only through a translation), so one test cannot cover both without a new\nobservation, which is exactly what that rule 
forbids.\n\n**MY Q3 HYPOTHESIS WAS REFUTED, and recording that matters as much as a finding.**  I said a 
third\nwrong-destination assignment was *\"more likely than not\"* given two in one function, and asked for the 
whole\nhandler to be read.  It was, and there is no third.  **A predicted class that does not materialise is 
a\nresult** -- and it stops the next round hunting for one.\n\n*** READ-ONLY AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, 
`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/codex_read_20260907.txt`).\nTHE DEFECT IS CONFIRMED FIELD BY FIELD, THE FIX IS ONE 
TOKEN, AND A PREMISE OF MINE ABOUT\nREACHABILITY WAS WRONG IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE BROKEN THE WITNESS. 
***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"`swpctx` assigns `apcb_ptbr` twice, leaves `apcb_flags` unchanged, and uses all nine\nlocals.  
Its return line writes `a0`.  Opcode-0 dispatch has no privilege check, but\n`alpha_palcode()` itself checks user mode 
before the switch.\"*\n\n**CONFIRMED, from the seat's own nine-row assignment table rather than from my 
description:**\n`apcb_ptbr` is assigned twice, `apcb_flags` never, and **all nine locals are used** -- so the 
defect\nis precisely a mis-typed destination, not a dropped load.  **The fix is ONE IDENTIFIER TOKEN on ONE\nLINE** 
(`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`), which the seat states explicitly.  The `a0`-for-`v0` return is\nconfirmed as a second, 
separate defect in the same handler.\n\n*** THE CORRECTION, AND IT IS A HARNESS-BREAKER RATHER THAN A RECORDS NICETY. 
***  I wrote -- in the\nbrief AND in the first draft of the Alpha harness -- that `call_pal` dispatch is 
unconditional.\n**Dispatch INTO `alpha_palcode()` is; the function then GATES ITS OWN SWITCH** 
at\n`cpu_alpha_palcode.c:183`:\n\n    uint64_t userMode = cpu->cd.alpha.ps & ALPHA_PSL_USERMODE;      /* 0x0008 */\n   
 if (userMode && !(palcode == 0x80 || 0x81 || 0x83 || 0xae ||\n                      0xaa || 0x86 || 0x9e || 0x92 || 
0x9f)) {\n            fatal(\"[ Privileged Alpha PALcode called from user mode: TODO ]\");\n            cpu->running = 
0;\n            return;\n    }\n\n**`0x30` is not on that whitelist**, so in user mode `call_pal 0x30` does not reach 
swpctx -- it\nHALTS THE CPU.  **That failure presents as a probe whose step never completes**, i.e. as a 
broken\nharness rather than as a mode problem, which is the worst kind of failure to debug.  Verified by\nreading the 
lines rather than taken on the seat's word.\n\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ps` has NO explicit initialiser anywhere in `src/`, so 
it is zero from the zeroed cpu\nstruct and USERMODE is clear -- kernel mode, and the gate does not fire.  **That is a 
derivation, and\nthe harness turns it into a measurement**: `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py` now carries a 
`palgate`\nrow asserting the diagnostic is absent, and a `palalive` row asserting the machine survives a swpctx\ncall 
at all.\n\n*Note the shape: the premise that was wrong is the one I did NOT think to question -- I checked 
the\ninstruction decoder carefully and never looked inside the function it dispatches to.  That is the\nthird instance 
this week and it is the subject of the queued rule question\n[[sgilinearnegidxpostmortem]], which asks whether the 
premise-audit instruction needs a\nself-directed twin.  **This is its third data point and the first where a seat 
caught it before it\ncost anything.***\n\n**AND THE BRIEF THAT ASKED THIS WAS THE SECOND ATTEMPT.**  The first -- 
carrying a four-step witness\nDESIGN -- was REFUSED by the same seat as chain-shaped content (see 
[[codexbriefscoping]]).  The\nchain-free re-ask, which requests a field-by-field audit and names no procedure, was 
answered in\nfull.  **The design question went to grok instead**, so neither question was dropped.\n\n*** THE WITNESS 
DESIGN WAS REFUTED, BY THE SEAT THE REFUSED BRIEF WAS ROUTED TO.  
grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_witness_20260907.txt`, 13.5 kB. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"YES, a rung-3 
witness is buildable on the three constructing Alpha machines -- but the\nproposed 'no MMU at all, two `swpctx`, read 
`A+16`' design is unsound as written if `A.flags` is\nnonzero.  After the first switch, `apcb_ptbr` IS the flags word, 
the identity map dies, and the\nsecond `call_pal` (and the save that is supposed to publish the bug) can fail to land 
or can\n`exit(1)` the host.  Use K0SEG for code and both PCBs, or keep `flags==0` and accept a weaker\noracle.  The 
one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the previous LHS.\"*\n\n*** THE DESIGN WAS SELF-DEFEATING 
AND I HAD NOT SEEN IT: THE DEFECT DESTROYS THE PATH THE WITNESS\nNEEDS TO OBSERVE IT. ***  Verified in 
`memory_alpha.c` rather than taken on the seat's word, and the\nsource is sharper than the verdict:\n\n  * **K0SEG is 
checked BEFORE `base` is used at all** (`:62`): `if (!userMode && vaddr >=\n    ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE && vaddr <= 
ALPHA_K0SEG_END) { *return_paddr = vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff; return\n    2; }`.  A K0SEG access never consults 
`apcb_ptbr`, so grok's remedy is sound.\n    `ALPHA_K0SEG_BASE = 0xfffffc0000000000`; `ALPHA_PHYS_TO_K0SEG(x) = x | 
BASE`.\n  * **The identity map is an explicit 'UGLY hack' conditioned on `base == 0`** (`:73`).  That is the\n    ONLY 
reason a naive harness works at all -- `ptbr` is zero at reset.  **The moment the buggy\n    assignment makes 
`apcb_ptbr` nonzero, `base != 0` and every later access falls into a real\n    page-table walk over garbage**, with an 
`exit(1)` sitting on one vaddr at `:75-78`.\n  * **AND THE POLARITY TRAP grok did not state, which kills the 'keep 
flags==0' fallback too:** if\n    the marker is put in `A.ptbr` instead, then under the *FIXED* build `apcb_ptbr` 
becomes that\n    nonzero marker and the identity map dies on the FIX rather than on the defect.  **Neither\n    
polarity survives with a nonzero value anywhere in that field.**  So K0SEG is not the better\n    option, it is the 
only one.\n\n**THE CORRECTED DESIGN, and it needs no 64-bit immediates:** `lda`/`ldah` build 32-bit constants\nonly, 
and a K0SEG address needs bit 63..42 set.  The way through is to **load the pointers BEFORE the\nfirst `swpctx`, while 
the identity map is still alive**: place the 64-bit K0SEG pointers in\nidentity-mapped scratch with the debugger's 
`put q`, have the guest `ldq` them into registers, and\nonly then issue the calls.  Every access after that carries a 
K0SEG address in a register and is\nimmune to whatever `apcb_ptbr` becomes.\n\n**A SEPARATE TRAP FOUND WHILE CHECKING 
THIS, which the harness avoids only by luck:** `memory_alpha.c:87`\nsilently remaps `(vaddr & ~0x1fff) == 0x20000000` 
to `PROM_ARGSPACE_PADDR` (0x12000).  The harness's\ncode at `0x20010000` and scratch at `0x20020000` fall OUTSIDE that 
8 kB window -- but nothing said\nso, and a probe placed 64 kB lower would have had its stores quietly land in PROM 
argument space.\n\n**Q4 ANSWERED:** *\"The one-token fix is not a PAL convention; it is a copy-paste of the 
previous\nLHS.\"*  Both seats agree the correction is `apcb_flags` at `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:282`.\n\n*** THE ROUTING 
WORKED AND IS THE REUSABLE LESSON. ***  codex REFUSED this brief as chain-shaped;\ngrok answered it in full, read the 
handler, the dispatch path and the existing harness, and refuted\nthe design on a mechanism I had missed.  **The 
chain-free re-ask to codex and the chain-shaped\noriginal to grok together cost one extra invocation and lost 
nothing** -- which is the argument the\n2026-09-09 adjudication is asked to rule on under 
[[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** CODE REVIEW OF THE HARNESS, BEFORE ITS FIRST RUN.  
grok,\n`regress/artifacts/alphaswpctxptbr/grok_harness_20260907.txt` -- codex REFUSED this brief too (sixth\nrefusal, 
chain/witness shape), so it is grok's third answer to a brief codex would not take. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DO NOT 
TRUST THE FIRST RUN.  The lda/ldah/ldq bootstrap and the swpctx SAVE/RESTORE\nwalk are right.  The encodings that this 
file actually emits match the decoder.  `put d` is the right\ndebugger command.  What is wrong is the ADDRESS PLAN, 
and it makes three rows able to go green while\nmeasuring 'unmapped reads return 0', not the handler.\"*\n\n*** 
FINDING 1 -- FATAL, AND IT WOULD HAVE BEEN SILENT AND GREEN. ***  The machine is built `-M 64`\n= 64 MB and every 
address in the file sat at `0x200xxxxx` (512 MB+).  K0SEG translation is\n`vaddr & 0x3ffffffffff`, so **the K0SEG idea 
survives contact with `alpha_translate_v2p` and the\nPHYSICAL BACKING does not.**  And the failure mode is 
silent:\n`memory_warn_about_unimplemented_addr` only halts when `halt_on_nonexistant_memaccess` is set, i.e.\nunder 
`-T`.  **Without it, writes succeed and are DISCARDED and reads RETURN ZEROS** -- and an\ninstruction fetch of zeros 
is a real instruction, `call_pal 0`.\n\n**MY ORACLE MADE THAT UNDETECTABLE.**  It asserted `PCB_A+16 == 0` for the 
correct case, and 0 is\nalso what an unmapped read returns.  **The witness would have gone green having written 
nothing,\nread nothing, and executed `call_pal 0` off a zero-filled code page.**\n\n**REPAIRED, and the repair is 
better than moving the addresses:** K0SEG means translation never\nconsults `apcb_ptbr`, so `apcb_ptbr` may now hold a 
distinctive NONZERO value -- which the FIRST\ndesign could not allow, because a nonzero ptbr killed the identity map.  
So:\n\n    correct handler  -> PCB_A+16 == PTBR_VAL  (0xaaaa1111)\n    the defect       -> PCB_A+16 == FLAGS_VAL 
(0xbbbb2222)\n    anything broken  -> 0, which is NEITHER, so BOTH rows red\n\nplus a third row `swpctx mapped` 
asserting the value is not 0 -- **because without it a probe whose\naddresses drifted back outside RAM would fail one 
row and pass the other, which reads exactly like a\nreal defect.**  Addresses moved inside 64 MB with an `assert` that 
says so.\n\n*** FINDING 2 -- `palalive` IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, AND I WROTE IT WHILE APPLYING THE RULE\nAGAINST 
THAT. ***  *\"`alive` is 'the pty is still readable'.  `alpha_palcode`'s default arm does\n`cpu->running = 0` and 
stays in the debugger because `-V` is single-step.  The emulator does not\nexit.  `palalive` stays green.\"*  **Kept 
as a LABELLED NON-ORACLE rather than deleted**, so the next\nreader sees why it is there and does not re-add it as a 
real row; the mode question is answered by\n`palgate`, which greps the diagnostic text.\n\n**FINDING 3 -- the encoder 
self-test does not test the decoder.**  It re-runs `load_const`'s own\n32-bit wrap masked with `& 0xffffffff`, while 
LDAH sign-extends through an `(int32_t)` cast --\n`0xdeadbeef` would be `0xffffffffdeadbeef` in a register and the 
self-test would still print ok.\n**It is sound only because every value this file uses has bit 31 clear.**  Correct by 
luck; recorded\nin the file as such rather than quietly fixed.\n\n**FINDING 4 -- `put q` is not merely 128-bit, it is 
UNIMPLEMENTED** (`\"quad-words: TODO\"`).  The\npremise was true and understated.\n\n**CONFIRMED, so the design work 
was not wasted:** encodings match the decoder; the bootstrap method\nis valid and nothing between the `put d` writes 
and the first `call_pal` touches `apcb_ptbr`; K0SEG\nis checked before `base`; PCB offsets match `struct alpha_pcb`; 
`ps` is zero from `zeroed_alloc`;\n`0x30` is not on the unprivileged whitelist.  It also supplied two facts I did not 
have:\n`cpu->cd.alpha.ctx` starts at `0x10100`, and PROM setup (on by default) sets `a0 = 2048`.\n\n*** THE REVIEW 
COST ONE INVOCATION AND SAVED A GREEN WITNESS THAT PROVED NOTHING. ***  The lifecycle\nsays ask a seat to try a rung-3 
probe; this says ask a seat to READ the probe before running it, and\non this evidence the second is worth as much as 
the first.\n\n*** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND RUN, 2026-09-07.  THE DEFECT REPRODUCES ON THE COMMITTED BUILD. ***\n\n    
row              got          want              \n    encoder          ok           ok                load_const sign 
correction, 9 vectors, offline\n    live             0x5a5a5a5a   0x5a5a5a5a        LIVENESS: guest store then load 
through RAM\n    exec             0x1234       0x1234            a guest LDAH/LDA pair reached a register\n    palgate 
         not gated    not gated         call_pal 0x30 is privileged; we are in kernel mode\n    palalive         alive 
       alive             NON-ORACLE, labelled as such\n    swpctx alive     alive        alive             the guest 
survives two swpctx calls in K0SEG\n    swpctx ptbr      0xbbbb2222   0xaaaa1111        FAIL -- the defect\n    swpctx 
notflags  0xbbbb2222   NOT 0xbbbb2222    FAIL -- the defect's exact signature\n    swpctx mapped    0xbbbb2222   NOT 
0x0           ok  -- the memory WAS there\n    ALPHAHARNESS_RESULT=7/9\n\n**THE MECHANISM, confirmed by the 
measurement rather than only by reading:** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`\ncase `0x30` LOADs the new context, sets `apcb_ptbr = 
new_ptbr`, and then **overwrites it with\n`new_flags` a few lines later**.  The probe stages PCB_A with ptbr 
`0xaaaa1111` and flags\n`0xbbbb2222`, swaps A in, swaps B in (whose SAVE writes A's context back to `PCB_A+16`), and 
reads\nthat cell.  It holds the FLAGS.\n\n*** `swpctx mapped` IS THE ROW THAT MAKES THIS A MEASUREMENT RATHER THAN A 
PHANTOM, and it exists\nbecause a code review demanded it. ***  The grok harness review found that **every address in 
the\nfirst draft was planted past the end of RAM** -- `-M 64` gives 64 MB and the structures sat at\n`0x20010000`+ 
(512 MB) -- where writes are discarded and reads return zeros with no diagnostic.\nThree rows would have gone GREEN 
while measuring *unmapped memory returns 0*.  The repair moved\neverything below 64 MB **and added `assert max(P_CODE, 
P_PTRV, P_PCB_A, P_PCB_B) < 64*1024*1024`**,\nso the class cannot be reintroduced silently.  `swpctx mapped` then pins 
the same property at run\ntime: a `0` readback is reported as a SETUP FAILURE, not as a result.\n\n> **THAT IS THE 
THIRD DISTINCT ROW-CLASS THIS SESSION WHOSE WHOLE PURPOSE IS TO SEPARATE 'MEASURED\n> NOTHING' FROM 'MEASURED A 
ZERO'** -- the UBSan in-band control, the kill-table NUL check, and this.\n> They were built independently and they 
are all the same rule: *an apparatus that cannot fail\n> loudly will fail quietly and be believed.*\n\n**TEST-FIRST IS 
SATISFIED:** the witness shows the symptom on the committed build BEFORE any edit,\nwhich is what licenses a fix.  The 
probe exits non-zero on a red row, so `rc=1` here is the correct\noutcome and not an error.\n\n**AND IT UNBLOCKS 
[[alphapfnoffset]]**, which is the reason this row was worked first: that row's\nwitness needs a non-zero `apcb_ptbr` 
and a valid page table, and *this* defect clobbers `apcb_ptbr`\nwith the flags word -- so any translation probe built 
before this was fixed could not attribute a\nbad translation to the walk rather than to the swap.  The confound is now 
measured rather than\nsuspected.\n\n*** A SECOND CONSEQUENCE THIS ROW DOES NOT RECORD, found by COUNTING the 
assignments rather than\nreading them.  2026-09-07. ***\n\nOver `cpu_alpha_palcode.c:274-292`, the load block's field 
assignments are:\n\n    apcb_ksp 1   apcb_usp 1   apcb_cpc 1   apcb_asn 1   apcb_unique 1\n    apcb_decrsv0 1   
apcb_decrsv1 1\n    apcb_ptbr  **2**        <- :278 = new_ptbr, :282 = new_flags\n    apcb_flags **0**        <- never 
assigned at all\n\n**So `:282` is a field-name transposition: it should be `apcb_flags = new_flags`.**  The value 
IS\nloaded -- the SAVE block stores `apcb_flags` to `ctx + 40` and the loader reads `new_flags` from\n`ctx + 40` -- 
and then lands in the wrong field.\n\n> **CONSEQUENCE 1 is the clobber the witness measures: `apcb_ptbr` ends up 
holding the flags word.**\n> **CONSEQUENCE 2 is new: `apcb_flags` is NEVER LOADED, so a context switch silently drops 
the\n> incoming context's flags.**  The row's title names only the first.\n\n*And the block's own comment at `:275` 
reads **\"Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously\"** --\nwhich is exactly the property it fails to have.  A 
comment asserting completeness directly above an\nincomplete list is the shape this project keeps finding: the claim 
is the thing least likely to be\nre-checked, because it reads as a description of the code below it.*\n\n**The 
counting is what found it.**  Reading the block, `:282` looks like an ordinary assignment in a\ncolumn of ordinary 
