>    "note": "\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase research -- moved out of the seat entries because the dashboard 
declares no such seat]  THIRD independent confirmation of the CONTENT rule, and the first scored by the POSITION-keyed 
detector shipped the day before.  sgirandomcolour/codex_scoped_20260908.err is a genuine CONTENT-FILTER REFUSAL: two 
markers IN THE TAIL, zero verdict blocks, the seat's own words being 'I can assess the code path and disposition, but 
I can't provide or run a guest sequence designed to reproduce the suspected defect.'  Counting the string would have 
been ambiguous here; position is not.  The routing fact holds: ask codex for the GAP, never for the WITNESS.  Measured 
the same day AGAINST a control: a GAP-shaped, four-file, premise-as-question brief (wiringcliff) returned a full 12.9 
KB verdict from the same seat in the same session.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 2026-09-09, phase research -- moved out of the 
seat entries because the dashboard declares no such seat]  A THIRD CODEX RULE, measured: the CONTENT rule (ask for the 
GAP, not the EDIT) is necessary and not sufficient -- an analysis-shaped brief was refused because the DIFF IT INLINED 
carried memory-safety language in its comments.  Two sibling briefs fired the same minute with the same shape and no 
such diff text (pcc2c040kill completeness; armbfxwidth32, whose diff is arithmetic) both answered in full.  RULE: for 
codex, a review of a memory-safety fix is pointed at the FILE (the ARGV_CAP pointer form) rather than inlined, or the 
diff is summarised -- and any brief that inlines a diff is scanned for the trigger vocabulary before firing.  The 
position-keyed detector caught it correctly; the SIZE (29 KB) would have read as an answer.\n\n[MAIN-LOOP NOTE, 
2026-09-10, phase research -- in `note` rather than in `entries` because the dashboard declares no main-loop seat, 
following this row's own 2026-09-09 precedent]\n\n*** A ZERO-BYTE CODEX ANSWER HAS A SECOND, INDEPENDENT CAUSE, AND 
THIS ROW'S OWN DETECTOR WOULD MISATTRIBUTE IT TO THE FILTER. ***\n\nTwo farmed briefs came back with 0-byte .txt files 
today.  The position-keyed detector this row shipped says REFUSAL, and for one of them it is right.  For the other the 
cause is in OUR LAUNCHER:\n\n    UnicodeDecodeError: 'charmap' codec can't decode byte 0x90 in position 7509\n\nThe 
inline launcher read codex stdout in TEXT mode, which on Windows decodes cp1252, and codex emits bytes cp1252 cannot 
map.  The wrapper died and wrote nothing.  **The carrier already records this exact trap for the Ollama seats -- 
\"write responses to utf-8 files, never print() (cp1252 crash)\" -- and it was never applied to the codex 
farm.**\n\nWHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN AN ORDINARY BUG: a 0-byte answer is the SAME OBSERVABLE for four different states 
-- terminal refusal, quota wall, run still in flight, and now wrapper crash.  A project that has just built a refusal 
detector will confidently read the fourth as the first.  That is the seat-failure-misattributed shape rather than the 
seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape, and it is harder to see because the detector AGREES with the wrong 
answer.\n\nFIXED at the source rather than in prose: the farm launcher now captures stdout as BYTES and decodes utf-8 
with errors=\"replace\", so no decode can throw and the answer survives whatever codex emits.\n\n*** AND THE POSITION 
DISCRIMINATOR GOT ITS CLEANEST CONTROL PAIR TODAY, IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. ***  The rule is that POSITION decides and 
COUNT does not, and here are two runs on the same day, same CLI, same effort:\n\n    codex_fixdesign_20260910   3 
flags at lines 2345/2540/2587 of 3987  (59-65% depth)  ->  10,731-byte ANSWER\n    codex_unrowed_20260910     8 flags, 
the LAST TWO at lines 18860-18861 of 18863      ->  0-byte, TERMINAL\n\n**A `grep -c` scores the healthy run as the 
WORSE refusal** -- 3 flags against 8 is the wrong way round only if you count, and the run with FEWER flags is the one 
that died.  Mid-stream flags are individual tool calls that codex retries around and they cost nothing; a flag in the 
last lines is the seat refusing.  This is the same POSITION-versus-SIZE discriminator seat_verdicts.py already uses to 
catch a wall in an otherwise healthy-looking 36 KB answer, arrived at independently for a different transport.\n\nONE 
MORE DATA POINT ON THE CONTENT RULE, and it is a NEGATIVE one worth recording: **the refused brief was ALREADY the 
benign one.**  brief_unrowed.md is written throughout in ordinary-defect-triage language (\"Ordinary defect triage on 
an emulator\"), with no offensive-security wording anywhere, and it was refused anyway.  **So benign FRAMING is not 
sufficient**, and the trigger is closer to specific vocabulary than to tone -- which sharpens this row's title claim 
rather than merely confirming it.\n\nSTATED AS OPEN RATHER THAN CLOSED, because the controlling measurement HAS NOT 
FINISHED: brief_unrowed2.md is a second rewrite of the same question that additionally drops the phrase 
\"guest-reachable exits\" and every row-id resembling a CVE handle, and reframes it as \"library code that calls 
exit() instead of returning\".  At the time of writing that run is STILL IN FLIGHT -- 5 flags, all mid-stream, no 
terminal pair, no \"tokens used\" line, codex.exe alive.  **If it lands with an answer that is the controlled A/B this 
row has never had** (same question, same seat, same day, vocabulary the only variable).  If it refuses in the tail, 
the vocabulary hypothesis is refuted and the trigger is the SUBJECT.  Either way the result belongs here; it is not 
being predicted.\n"
