     "opened": "2026-09-10",
     "title": "cpu_ppc_instr.c X(rldicl) ALWAYS WRITES ZERO: the mask is accumulated into `tmp' while the destination 
is assigned `tmp & tmp2' and tmp2 is never anything but 0 -- one of the most common 64-bit PowerPC instructions 
returns 0 for every input",
>    "note": "FILED 2026-09-10.  Found by the GPT-6 Astra seat under the found-something-else licence while answering 
a tree-wide SHIFT-UB census -- it says so itself: *'one definite additional finding deserves its own correctness row 
... This is CRITICAL and EASY, independently of the shift-UB census.'*  **VERIFIED LOCALLY by opening the handler 
before filing.**\n\n*** THE DEFECT, and it is four lines you can hold in your head. ***\n    uint64_t tmp = 
cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[rs], tmp2;\n    while (sh-- != 0) { ... rotate tmp left by one ... }\n    tmp2 = 0;\n    while (mb <= 
63) {\n            tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-mb));      <-- the MASK goes into tmp\n            mb ++;\n    }\n    
cpu->cd.ppc.gpr[ra] = tmp & tmp2;             <-- tmp2 is STILL 0\n`tmp2' is assigned 0 and never assigned again, so 
**the destination register is set to zero on every execution, for every operand, every rotate amount and every mask 
begin.**  The `|=' also corrupts the rotated value it lands in, but that is moot: the result is ANDed with zero either 
way.\n\n*** THE SIBLING TWO FUNCTIONS DOWN SHOWS THE INTENDED SHAPE, which is what makes the diagnosis certain rather 
than inferred. ***  X(rldicr) does the same rotate and then CLEARS bits in `tmp' (`tmp &= ~((uint64_t)1 << (63-me))'), 
assigning `tmp' directly.  X(rldicl) was written the other way round -- BUILD a mask, then AND -- and the mask was 
accumulated into the wrong variable.  The fix is one character: `tmp |=' becomes `tmp2 |='.\n\n*** WHY THIS IS THE 
MOST SERIOUS THING IN TODAY'S BATCH. ***  `rldicl' is not an obscure corner: it is THE canonical 64-bit PowerPC 
masking and zero-extension instruction, emitted constantly by compilers (`rldicl rA,rS,0,32' is the standard 32-bit 
zero-extend).  Every other defect filed today needs a guest to touch a particular register or width; this one is wrong 
on the ordinary path, for ordinary code, on every execution.\n\nREACHABILITY IS WIRED AND CHECKED: `case 
PPC_30_RLDICL: ic->f = instr(rldicl); break;' in the primary decoder, reached whenever `cpu->cd.ppc.bits == 64' -- the 
32-bit path deliberately goes to `bad' instead.  So it is live on every 64-bit PowerPC CPU model this tree offers, and 
dead on the 32-bit ones.\n\n*** AND IT IS NOT A SHIFT DEFECT, which matters for how the round is scoped: the census 
explicitly records the computed count here as SAFE (mask counts 0..63, rotate counts 1..31, form NONE). *** This is a 
plain variable mix-up that the census found while looking at something else.  It does NOT belong to [[shiftubcensus]] 
or [[mipsextshift]]; it is its own correctness round.\n\nNOT MEASURED and no witness built.  The rung-3 attempt is 
owed FIRST and looks unusually cheap: any 64-bit PowerPC machine, one `rldicl' with a nonzero source, and a register 
readback -- the wanted value is anything but zero, and the pre-fix build must show zero.  A guest-visible wrong RESULT 
is a far easier oracle than the process-death oracles most of this queue needs.  **Check first whether the existing 
PPC gates already execute one and pass anyway**, because a green gate over an always-zero instruction would itself be 
a finding about the gates.\n\n*** WITNESSED AT RUNG 3, 2026-09-10, AND MY FILING UNDERSTATED IT IN THREE WAYS. 
***\n\n(a) **TWO TOKENS ARE WRONG, NOT ONE, AND THE OBVIOUS REPAIR WAS BUILT AND MEASURED STILL WRONG.**  The mask is 
ORed into `tmp', the ROTATED VALUE, so even setting the AND aside the data is destroyed by having every mask bit 
forced to 1.  `tmp2' never being written is a CONSEQUENCE of the same slip rather than an independent bug.  The seat 
built both candidates: the one-character `tmp |=' -> `tmp2 |=' measures CORRECT at 0 of 190 wrong, while DELETING the 
`& tmp2' -- the edit a future reader is most likely to reach for -- ships an OR where the architecture says AND and 
gets 126 of 190 cells wrong.  **It is worse than the present bug in one specific way: it removes the always-zero 
signature that makes the defect easy to spot while leaving the value wrong.**  Recorded so the next round does not 
take it.\n\n(b) **A SECOND OBSERVABLE CHANNEL: IT CORRUPTS CONTROL FLOW, NOT ONLY DATA.**  update_cr0 is fed the 
destination, which is always zero, so the record form reports EQ on EVERY execution regardless of operands -- measured 
0x20000000 committed against 0x40000000 fixed.  Any branch on a masked compare takes the wrong arm.  A detector owes a 
row on this channel.\n\n(c) **IT IS REACHABLE ON A REAL MACHINE MODEL, not only a synthetic one.**  All three in-tree 
machines that select a 64-bit CPU return zero: bareppc, testppc, and macppc -e g5 -- the Power Mac G5, whose 
constructor gives it the PPC970.\n\nTHE ORACLE CAME FROM THE MANUAL, NOT FROM THIS TREE: the rldiclx RTL in 
ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt plus its prose, with field placement taken from the manual's own format diagram and then 
independently confirmed by inverting the handler's extraction expressions.  Deriving the wants from the sibling would 
have been weaker and from the handler itself circular.  196 of 196 encoded words round-tripped through THE TREE'S OWN 
DISASSEMBLER, a different code path from the execute handler -- the defence against this project's 
hand-assembled-encoding trap, and it matters here because a wrong register field yields zero and zero is the symptom 
under test.\n\nTHE CENSUS IS NON-VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION, which is what makes 190 of 190 a real number: the source is 
all-ones, so the rotate is the identity and the architectural answer is exactly the mask, which is non-empty for every 
mask-begin in 0..63.  **And the seat measured a genuinely VACUOUS cell to show the boundary**: with a source whose 
selected window is all zeros the correct answer is itself zero and the row is GREEN on the broken build.  The rule for 
a detector author is to choose a source where every mask window is non-empty.\n\n*** THE GATE FINDING IS STRUCTURAL 
AND STRONGER THAN 'NO GATE COVERS IT'. ***  Both PowerPC gates drive macppc -e g4, an MPC7400, which the CPU table 
marks 32-BIT -- and the decoder sends every rld* to the bad path in 32-bit mode.  **So no gate has ever instantiated a 
64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, and the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible to the gates BY CONSTRUCTION 
rather than by omission.**  That is better than a green gate passing over a broken instruction, which is what I had 
asked to be checked.  It is one coverage hole with one cause, and a single 64-bit probe closes all of it.\n\nSEVERITY 
CEILING, stated honestly rather than glossed: the only PowerPC kernel image in the tree is loaded onto a G4 and never 
executed, so nothing in-tree BOOTS a 64-bit PowerPC guest.  The defect is catastrophic for any such guest and there is 
not currently one, which is why the witness is rung 3 and why it cannot be rung 4.\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND 
IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT 
(wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU 
at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 
(gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, 
all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all 
along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION 
check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and 
their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES 
these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS 
PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine 
CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself 
executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when 
reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE 
PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into 
gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It 
does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it 
does.",
     "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/opus_ppc_rldicl_witness_20260910.py -- RUNG 3 on unmodified -E 
bareppc (and testppc, and macppc -e g5), real instructions through real decode and execution, nothing edited to reach 
the site.  COMMITTED 19/27 with 190 of 190 census cells wrong and all 190 returning zero; FIX 24/27 with 0 of 190 
wrong.  Logs witness_committed.out / witness_fix.out / witness_naive.out, each verified by its PPC_RLDICL_WITNESS_DONE 
sentinel.  SHIPPED DETECTOR: regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py, 24 rows, killtable_20260910/t2/SCORES.txt -- BASE 24/24, the 
whole-file PRE-FIX leg 16/24 [clause (a); labelled 'null medium' until 2026-10-09 -- an L4 per 7n-1; clause (c) is 
unmeasured and OWED, row nullmediumlegs], EIGHT mutants 16..23/24, ZERO ESCAPES, restore verified on both parts.  The 
two mutants that escaped the 19-row form (M_SH5, M_CR0) are each killed ONLY by the rows added for them.",
     "closure": {
      "by": "opus5-mainloop",
      "badge": "A",
      "note": "FIXED AND SHIPPED AS #522.  X(rldicl) wrote ZERO to its destination on every execution: the mask was 
ORed into the ROTATED VALUE while the destination got `tmp & tmp2' and tmp2 was never anything but 0.  ONE TOKEN fixes 
it, and it repairs both halves because the `|=' had also destroyed the rotated data.  This is the canonical 64-bit 
masking and zero-extension instruction, so it was wrong on the ordinary path for ordinary compiler output.  WITNESSED 
at rung 3 before the edit, 19/27 -> 24/27, with a 190-cell census in which every cell was wrong and every cell 
returned exactly zero.  *** PASS 2 RETURNED BREACH-FOUND WITH TWO MEASURED TOTAL ESCAPES, both repaired in-round: a 
shift-decode mutant the instrument was blind to BY CONSTRUCTION (the census uses an all-ones source, so rotating it is 
the identity, and the only rotate-sensitive rows drove shifts below 32 while every logical right shift uses 32 or 
more); and a condition-register mutant that is NOT INVENTED -- it is the token the sibling handler ships correctly ten 
lines below. ***  Three rows close both, each the sole killer of its mutant, all three also reddening under the defect 
and the null medium.  FIVE RECORDS were wrong and are corrected, including a citation in PRODUCT CODE and a detector 
row that cannot fail under any mutation.  The apparatus failed twice and both failures are preserved rather than 
deleted -- one of them looked HALF-HEALTHY because two mutators share a mutant name.  Two sibling defects in the same 
family are filed, measured and unfixed; a gate claim of mine was false and is corrected in place."
     },
     "sev": "high",
     "opened": "2026-09-10",
     "title": "cpu_ppc_instr.c X(rldimi): the rotate loop's post-decrement leaves sh == -1, so the insert mask always 
runs to bit 63 instead of 63-SH -- the instruction OVERWRITES the 64-SH destination bits the architecture requires it 
to PRESERVE, for every SH != 0 [CORRECTED 2026-10-18 at the #527 closure (GPT-6 Astra): the bit counts are wrong -- 
for a non-wrapping mask the defect overwrote the LOW SH bits that must survive, and for a wrapping mask it omitted the 
required insertion into positions 0..63-SH (OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md, `ppcrldimimask` DESIGNED 2026-09-10).]",
>    "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a 
byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW 
(regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; 
ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rldimi_probe.py (27 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a 
private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and 
target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections 
designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two 
new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL 
UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. *** 
[2026-10-18, at the #527 closure (Grok 4.7): DONE before #527 committed -- kill table 3 (killtable3_20260912/), the 
gate log (gate_ppc_20260912.log, PASS 110 checks) and the gate's eleven negative-control cases, ten expected to FAIL 
and one GOOD baseline (gate_negctl_20260912.out).  History, not open debt.]\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the 
[[ppcrldiclzero]] witness's sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler rotates with a `while (sh-- != 0)' 
loop, which ALWAYS exits with sh == -1 because the post-decrement runs once more than the body.  The mask loop then 
terminates on a condition involving 63 - sh, i.e. 64 -- always.  The architecture requires the mask to END AT 63-SH 
(the rldimix RTL and its prose, '1 bits from bit MB through bit 63 - SH').\n\nMEASURED with the destination seeded to 
0 and an all-ones source:\n    SH=8    got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffffffffff00\n    SH=32   got 
0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffff00000000\n    SH=0    got 0xffffffffffffffff   want 0xffffffffffffffff   -- the 
one safe case\n\n**This is the standard bitfield INSERT.**  A compiler using it to splice a field destroys everything 
to the right of that field.  Live for every SH != 0.\n\n*** THE DURABLE REPAIR IS NOT TO PATCH THE 63 - sh EXPRESSION. 
***  That loop idiom is shared by rldicl, rldicr AND rldimi; rldicr survives only because it never reads sh again.  
**Any future edit that reaches for sh after the rotate loop in any of the three will be wrong the same way.**  Save sh 
before the loop instead.\n\nSame reachability and the same gate blind spot as [[ppcrldiclzero]]: 64-bit CPUs only, and 
no gate instantiates one.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_design_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at 
max, 14,947 bytes, ZERO cyber flags): THE DEFECT PREMISE IS EXACT AND **MY PROPOSED REPAIR IS INSUFFICIENT**. 
***\n\nThe post-decrement reading is confirmed exactly: the loop's final test evaluates the old value zero and then 
decrements to -1, **and this happens even for SH=0**, so the mask always covers architectural bits MB..63.  The 
rldimix RTL requires MASK(MB, 63-SH).\n\n**BUT 'SAVE sh BEFORE THE LOOP AND KEEP THE MASK LOOP' -- which I filed as 
the durable repair -- LEAVES TWO MORE DEFECTS.**  Both come from the manual's own mask definition, which makes BOTH 
endpoints inclusive and WRAPS through bit 63 to bit 0 when the beginning exceeds the end:\n  (1) the existing loop 
compares the INCREMENTED position against the inclusive endpoint, so where it terminates normally it **stops one bit 
early**;\n  (2) it **never wraps**, so other operand combinations run past bit 63 and evaluate invalid shift counts -- 
an undefined-behaviour defect hiding inside the wrong-value one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED REPAIR is therefore four 
coordinated changes rather than one: preserve the decoded `sh', rotate with a separate counter (`int n = sh; while 
(n-- != 0)'), advance the mask position modulo 64 (`m = (m + 1) & 63'), and terminate on the position AFTER the 
endpoint, modulo 64 (`m != ((64 - sh) & 63)').\n\n*A round that had taken my one-line framing would have shipped a fix 
that is still wrong at the endpoint and still undefined on the wrapping cases, and its detector -- built from the same 
framing -- would very likely have passed it.*\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO 
THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT 
(wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU 
at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 
(gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, 
all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all 
along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION 
check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and 
their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES 
these instructions and inspects the answer -- not because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS 
PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine 
CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself 
executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when 
reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE 
PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into 
gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It 
does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so explicitly rather than letting the round claim it 
does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 (ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags).  MY 
PREMISES HELD AND MY CHARACTERISATION OF THE DEFECT WAS HALF OF IT. ***\n\nCONFIRMED: `sh' is `-1' on exit for EVERY 
decoded SH including zero (the final test reads the old value, decrements, and exits), the rotate body IS a correct 
ROTL64, and the mask loop therefore always stops at `m == 64' and selects bits `MB..63'.  The architectural mask is 
`MASK(MB, 63-SH)', INCLUSIVE of both endpoints and WRAPPING -- RTL at `ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:29908' (`m <- MASK(b, 
~n)'), endpoint prose at `:29909', mask definition at `:6879'.\n\n*** THE ROW'S TITLE DESCRIBES ONLY THE NON-WRAPPING 
HALF.  The defect has TWO directions: ***\n  * `MB <= 63-SH' (non-wrapping) -- it OVERWRITES the low SH destination 
bits that must survive;\n  * `MB > 63-SH' (wrapping) -- it OMITS the required insertion into positions `0..63-SH', 
leaving\n    those destination bits UNCHANGED.\nA repair tested only on non-wrapping encodings fixes one and leaves 
the other.\n\n**AND SAVING SH IS NOT ENOUGH -- THREE MORE EDITS ARE NEEDED, which is why my sketch was rejected.**  
The comparison happens AFTER the increment, so the stopping position is the endpoint's SUCCESSOR, and the counter must 
be circular:\n\n        int m, n = sh;                       /*  1: keep SH  */\n        while (n-- != 0) { ... }      
       /*  2: consume the copy  */\n        do {\n                tmp |= ((uint64_t)1 << (63-m));\n                m = 
(m + 1) & 63;            /*  3: circular increment  */\n        } while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));     /*  4: successor, 
modulo 64  */\n\n**Changing only one of edits 3 and 4 does not repair the loop.**  A partial repair that saves SH and 
sets the bound to 24 at `MB=40, SH=40' while keeping `m ++' walks 40..63 and then enters the body with `m == 64', 
evaluating `(uint64_t)1 << -1'.  *** That is UNDEFINED BEHAVIOUR, and the seat declined to call it an infinite loop on 
the ground that doing so would assign DEFINED behaviour to an undefined program -- which is the correct discipline and 
is what my brief's question got wrong. ***\n\nWORKED CASE, usable as a row: `rldimi r4,r3,40,40' = instruction word 
**0x7864422e**, with `r3 = 0xffffffffffffffff' and `r4 = 0': the repaired loop visits 40..63 then 0..23, 48 
iterations, mask **0xffffff0000ffffff**, which is also the correct result.  **The original handler produces 
0x0000000000ffffff.**\n\nMUTANT, one character, defined result: `64` -> `63` in the repaired bound.  `rldimi 
r4,r3,0,0' = **0x7864000c**, `r3 = ~0', `r4 = 0' gives correct `0xffffffffffffffff' and mutant `0xfffffffffffffffe' -- 
it drops the inclusive endpoint, architectural bit 63.\n\nThe seat validated its replacement against an independent 
bit-membership oracle over **40,960 cases** (every SH/MB pair x ten source/destination pairs), max loop count 64.  
**It could NOT compile or run the emulator** -- no native compiler, and WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED' -- so this is a 
READING plus an offline model, not a measurement of this tree.  A measuring seat still owes the build.\n\n**BLOCKER 
PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is corrected -- it was returning an all-zero 
mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*\"It is not possible to specify an all-zero 
mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  `mask32' is deliberately left alone pending 
[[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed build is still owed before either repair is 
scored.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s two corrected helpers were 
verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `& 63`, the all-zero-mask 
assertion's normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round 
without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY THE agy SEAT 
(owner-approved use of the measurement reserve).  WITNESS TEST-FIRST, FOUR-EDIT REPAIR APPLIED, KILL TABLE WITH **ZERO 
ESCAPES**. ***\n\n**THE WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME**, not asserted: 
`opus_ppc_rldimi_witness_20260911.py` and `witness_committed.out` at 23:02, `witness_fix.out` at 23:06, the repair 
after.\n\n    committed build (HEAD)   17/27   -- 10 failing rows, 431 of 495 census cells wrong\n    repaired build   
        27/27   -- 0 failing rows, 0 of 495 census cells wrong\n\n**THE REPAIR IS THE FOUR-EDIT FORM, BYTE-FOR-BYTE AS 
DESIGNED AND AS I VERIFIED IT OFFLINE OVER ALL 4096 (MB,SH) PAIRS** -- `int m, n = sh;` / `while (n-- != 0)` / `m = (m 
+ 1) & 63;` / `while (m != ((64 - sh) & 63));`.\n\n*** KILL TABLE, `killtable_20260911/SCORES.txt`, frozen instrument, 
one clone, sentinel dropped before mutant 1 and CLEARED ONLY AFTER BOTH CLEARANCE PARTS (source `cmp` CLEAN, binary 
FRESH): ***\n\n    BASE           27/27      M_NOKEEP_SH    16/27\n    M_ORIG         17/27      M_NOCIRC       
19/27\n    M_MUT63        14/27      M_NO_MERGE     24/27\n    M_NOWRAP       21/27      HEAD           17/27\n    
RESTORED       27/27\n\n**ZERO ESCAPES, AND EACH OF THE FOUR EDITS HAS ITS OWN MUTANT AND EACH IS KILLED** -- 
`M_NOKEEP_SH` for edits 1-2, `M_NOCIRC` for edit 3, `M_MUT63` and `M_NOWRAP` for edit 4.  *That is the sole-killer 
discipline applied to a repair's parts rather than to a detector's rows, and it is what makes \"four coordinated 
edits\" a measured claim rather than a description.*\n\n**TWO CONTROLS WORTH NAMING:** `HEAD` scores exactly 
`M_ORIG`'s 17/27 with the identical failing set, confirming the committed build IS the defect and the mutator 
reproduces it; and `M_NO_MERGE` reds ONLY the three preservation rows (`pres nowrap`, `pres wrap`, `pres sh0`), which 
is those rows earning their place.\n\n*** APPARATUS NOTE, AND IT IS THE REASON THIS REPORT NEARLY WENT UNREAD: agy's 
`--print-timeout 30m` EXPIRED WHILE ITS BACKGROUND TASKS WERE STILL RUNNING. ***  The print session returned 8.8 KB of 
progress narration and no report, with stderr reading *\"root agent idle; waiting for 2 background task(s)\"*.  **The 
report existed on disk the whole time** -- `opus_measure_20260911.md`, 13 KB -- and was found only by looking in the 
artifacts directory rather than at the seat's stdout.  *A build-plus-kill-table does not fit a 30-minute print budget; 
size the timeout to the job, and when an agy run ends with background tasks outstanding, GO AND READ THE ARTIFACTS.  
Its stderr is the tell and it is not the documented 111-byte quota wall.*\n\nSTILL OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS: **pass 2 on 
the diff**, per the repair-chain rule -- one pass per floor-bound diff, closing on the first pass that finds zero 
breaches.\n\n*** PASS 2 (PARTIAL -- ONE SEAT, AND THE MISS IS NAMED): NO INCORRECT RESULT FOUND, ZERO FLOOR BREACHES, 
TWO CONCRETE CLAUSE-(e) ITEMS. ***  `ppcrldimimask/codex_pass2_20260911.txt`.\n\n**THE INERT-REPAIR CHECK PASSES, AND 
IT WAS THE ONE I MOST WANTED.**  Yesterday an adopted design in this same file turned out inert because a loop mutated 
the variable its guard tested before the guard ran.  Here the dataflow is `decode sh -> initialise n from sh -> 
decrement n during rotation -> read the ORIGINAL sh in the mask bound`, with **no intervening assignment, increment, 
decrement or address escape**.  The bound depends on the preserved `sh`, not the exhausted `n`.  **The repair is 
ACTIVE.**\n\n**TERMINATION IS PROVED FOR EVERY ENCODING, including the case I flagged.**  At `(MB,SH) = (0,0)` the 
bound is `0` and the mask loop runs **exactly 64 times** -- a full circuit, which is correct, because `MASK(0,63)` is 
all-ones and a `do/while` treats initial equality as a complete circuit rather than zero iterations.  Generally the 
loop runs 1..64 times, the increment stays in `0..63`, and no mask-bit shift has an invalid count.\n\n*** TWO 
CLAUSE-(e) ITEMS, BOTH CONCRETE, AND THE FIRST IS THE KIND A VALUE ORACLE CANNOT SEE BY CONSTRUCTION: ***\n  1. **A UB 
rewrite that produces every expected value.**  Replacing the rotation loop with `s = (s << sh) | (s >> (64 - sh));` 
invokes undefined behaviour at `SH = 0`, where the right shift has count 64 -- **and a compiler that recognises the 
idiom and emits a hardware rotate yields every correct test value anyway.**  *A value-checking instrument cannot 
distinguish correct output from undefined behaviour that happens to produce correct output.*\n  2. **Register 
aliasing.**  Computing the mask first and delaying the source load until after the destination is cleared preserves 
behaviour when `RA != RS` and breaks `RA == RS`.  That is the same aliasing axis named for the `rlwinm` work, and it 
applies here too.\n\n**AND A COVERAGE QUALIFICATION ON MY OWN TABLE:** `M_NOKEEP_SH` removes edits 1 and 2 TOGETHER, 
so that leg does **not** establish separate runtime kills for the two textual changes.  My claim that *\"each of the 
four edits has its own mutant\"* is therefore true of edits 3 and 4 and loose about 1 and 2.\n\n*** THIS IS A PARTIAL 
PASS AND I AM RECORDING IT AS ONE. ***  It was a SINGLE SEAT, fired because the measuring seat was mid-build on 
another round and a nine-seat panel would have contended with it.  The repair-chain rule closes a chain on the first 
pass finding zero breaches **provided the pass is valid -- roster complete or its misses named**.  The misses are 
named here: eight seats unfired.  **The round owes its full panel before it ships**, and the seat itself noted it was 
not given the probe rows and so cannot claim the instrument lacks alias checks.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **the 
existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it appears verbatim 
in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c 
'^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` shows no change since `e777ff0`.  
`ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 
is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- `regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 
19/27`, and the superseded `pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a 
row-count column is the frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed 
gate-wiring closure, so a future round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check 
would pass while testing less than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11: CLAUSE (c) NOW GENUINELY DISCHARGED -- AN HONEST 
IDENTITY NULL MEDIUM, AND IT IS STRICTLY SHARPER THAN THE PRE-FIX LEG IT REPLACES. 
***\n\n`killtable_nullmedium_20260911/` (sentinel `NULLMEDIUM_DONE`), built by agy 3.8 in `/tmp/gxrldimi_clone` 
against the frozen instrument:\n\n    NULL_MEDIUM (identity X(rldimi) body)   SCORE 13/27   -- FOURTEEN rows RED\n    
RESTORED                                SCORE 27/27\n    Clearance: Part 1 (cmp vs SEC)=CLEAN    Part 2 (binary -nt 
source)=FRESH\n\nThe reds cover every substantive family -- `nowrap` x5, `wrap` x4, `pres` x3 -- **and both census 
rows (`census wrong`, `census zero`) also redden**, so they are not the pure bookkeeping I had predicted they might 
be.  *A prediction I wrote into the brief, refuted by the measurement, which is the right way round.*\n\n**THE 
COMPARISON IS THE FINDING, AND IT IS THE SECOND INSTANCE OF ONE PATTERN:**\n\n    ppcrldimimask   identity 13/27 (14 
red)   pre-fix HEAD leg 17/27 (10 red)\n    ppcrlwimiwidth  identity 12/28 (16 red)   pre-fix HEAD leg 14/28 (14 
red)\n\n**Both rounds, independently: the identity medium reddens MORE rows than the pre-fix medium the convention 
substitutes for it.**  That is what a sharper clause-(c) leg looks like, measured rather than argued -- and it is now 
carried to the flagship seat as question 7n-1, which asks whether *a strictly sharper form was available and cheap* 
converts an established house convention into a floor breach across the ~16 tables that use it.\n\n*The old discharge 
was `PRETABLE.out:69`, \"(c) null medium -> HEAD leg (whole-file pre-fix)\", with the PRETABLE itself predicting that 
leg identical to `M_ORIG` and the run confirming both at 17/27 -- two legs measuring one thing.  That sentence is 
superseded, not deleted: the leg's score was real; what was false was its NAME.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 
Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** 
***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration is unverified, and the probe covers neither 
fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by argument:** independent in-memory models 
found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand 
checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  *Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not 
compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` IS CORRECT.**  Book I's RTL is `m <- MASK(b, !n)` with 
`n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in 
prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT 
CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n> *'There is no shared functioning detector here -- the 
permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.'*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`'s two expected-value helpers exist but implement 
DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity 
floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes instruction-specific observations that kill its own 
effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO 
COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS **AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the 
designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows 
the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion.  *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 
'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE 
HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound as a representation argument, not an accident**: 
assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift 
itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the low-word insertion.\n\n> *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): 
both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is 
MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes 
the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which 
implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined to certify it** -- *'If a 64-bit processor's 32-bit 
execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high word, that remains wrong.'*  **Filed rather than 
guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the 
high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's 
more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` 
(see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so 
the next round does not invent the opposite convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and 
the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
     "witness": "probe:regress/artifacts/ppcrldimimask/opus_ppc_rldimi_witness_20260911.py",
     "entries": [
      {
       "seat": "codex",
       "phase": "review",
       "d": "SURVIVOR-SKIP-FULL-ROT",
     "sev": "high",
     "opened": "2026-09-10",
     "title": "cpu_ppc_instr.c X(rlwimi): the 32-bit rotate is performed at 64-bit width in the 64-bit build, so 
HIGH-HALF source data leaks into the low 32 bits -- its two siblings truncate to uint32_t first and this one never 
does",
>    "note": "*** 2026-09-12, codex farm: the promotion was NOT done -- regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py was a 
byte-identical copy of the witness (cmp).  Promoted per codex's design and REVIEW 
(regress/artifacts/ppcrlwimiwidth/codex_promote*_20260912.txt, codex_promoreview_20260912.txt; 
ppcrldimimask/codex_promote_20260912.txt): regress/ppc_rlwimi_probe.py (28 rows) with RESULT/CONTROL/DONE lines, a 
private loader stub, census wants pinned to MEASURED literals (they had been derived from the lists they count), and 
target-instruction CONTROL rows (without them a no-op instruction still read CONTROL=OK).  gate_ppc.sh sections 
designed to run BEFORE the NetBSD prerequisite with a strict parse -- PARKED at _scratchpad/gate_ppc.ppcround.sh (two 
new pins would raise SCORE_CLAIMS 25 -> 27) and MANIFEST entries parked (_scratchpad/ppc_manifest.py apply).  ALL 
UNMEASURED.  OWED: run both, re-table against frozen copies (rldimi M_NOCIRC by name), gate negative controls. *** 
[2026-10-18, at the #527 closure (Grok 4.7): DONE before #527 committed -- kill table 3 (killtable3_20260912/), the 
gate log (gate_ppc_20260912.log, PASS 110 checks) and the gate's eleven negative-control cases, ten expected to FAIL 
and one GOOD baseline (gate_negctl_20260912.out).  History, not open debt.]\n\nFILED 2026-09-10 from the 
[[ppcrldiclzero]] witness's sibling audit.  MEASURED, not read.\n\nThe handler takes its operands as MODE_uint_t and 
then rotates with a shift pair using 32-sh.  cpu_ppc.c defines DYNTRANS_DUALMODE_32, so the file is compiled TWICE and 
MODE_uint_t is **uint64_t** in the 64-bit build.  The architecture specifies a 32-bit rotate over the LOW half of the 
source -- **the high half plays no part whatsoever** -- but the right-shift pulls bits out of the high half straight 
into the low 32 bits, which is exactly where the mask lives.\n\nMEASURED, source 0xAAAAAAAA0000000F, destination 
seeded 0x5A5A..., shift 4, full mask:\n    got 0x5a5a5a5aaaaaaaf0    want 0x5a5a5a5a000000f0\nThe 0xAAAAAA is 
high-half data the architecture says cannot reach there.\n\nATTRIBUTION IS PINNED BY TWO COMPANION ROWS: rlwinm on the 
SAME source and shift is CORRECT, because it casts to uint32_t before rotating, and rlwimi at SH=0 is correct because 
the ternary short-circuits the rotate entirely.  **So the defect is the missing truncation in this one handler, not 
the shape of the rotate expression.**\n\n*A row testing this is VACUOUS unless the source's two halves DIFFER: the 
seat's own first draft used an all-ones source, under which a 64-bit rotate and a true 32-bit one agree bit for bit, 
and it corrected the row rather than shipping it green.*\n\nADJACENT, NOT A DEFECT BUT WORTH RECORDING so a round does 
not rediscover it: rldic, rldcl and rldcr are NOT IMPLEMENTED AT ALL -- the decoder's default swallows them.  rldic in 
particular is documented and compiler-emitted.\n\n*** DESIGN REVIEW 2026-09-10: THE WIDTH PREMISE HOLDS AND 
**TRUNCATION ALONE IS NOT THE FIX**. ***\n\nCasting the source to `uint32_t' before the rotate is necessary and not 
sufficient.  The prescribed repair splits the combined declaration -- `uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)' with the 
destination kept at the mode-dependent width -- **and additionally handles the WRAPPING mask**, because the manual's 
mask wraps when the beginning exceeds the end: `if (mb > me) ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);' AFTER 
the rotation and BEFORE the existing mask loop.\n\n*** AND A NEW FINDING THAT CONTRADICTS THE EARLIER SIBLING AUDIT: 
`rlwinm' AND `rlwnm' ARE NOT UNIVERSALLY CORRECT. ***  The witness measured them green on the cases it drove and the 
audit recorded them as correct; the design review says that is true of those cases and not of the handlers.  **A 
measured-green sample is not a proof of correctness**, which is this project's own reading-versus- measurement 
distinction cutting the other way for once -- the measurement was right about what it measured and the GENERALISATION 
from it was mine.  Re-check both before either is called clean, and do not let this row's fix be copied to them on the 
strength of the audit.\n\n*** THE GATE CLAIM WAS WRONG AND IS CORRECTED HERE.  I WROTE IT INTO THREE ROWS, A CHANGELOG 
BLOCK AND TWO COMMIT MESSAGES BEFORE A SEAT CHECKED IT (wiringcliff/codex_ppcgate_20260910.txt). ***\n\n**I CLAIMED: 
'no gate in this tree instantiates a 64-bit PowerPC CPU at all, so the whole 64-bit-only instruction group is 
invisible BY CONSTRUCTION.'  THE FIRST HALF IS FALSE.**  Gate 9 (gate_asan_sweep.sh) enumerates EVERY advertised 
machine type and subtype -- including bareppc, testppc and macppc/g5, all of which default to the PPC970 -- so 64-bit 
PowerPC CPUs ARE constructed by a gate today, and have been all along.\n\n**THE CORRECT AND NARROWER FINDING: NO GATE 
CHECKS THEIR INSTRUCTION RESULTS.**  Gate 9 is a CONSTRUCTION check with no PowerPC result oracle; the two PowerPC 
gates both drive `macppc -e g4', an MPC7400, which is 32-bit, and their probes select it EXPLICITLY rather than by 
default.  So the defects were invisible because nothing EXECUTES these instructions and inspects the answer -- not 
because nothing builds the CPU.\n\n*** THAT DISTINCTION IS THIS PROJECT'S OWN WITNESS LADDER, ONE LEVEL UP, AND I 
WALKED PAST IT. ***  The ladder's whole rung-2 clause says machine CONSTRUCTION proves presence and NOT reachability, 
and is a reproduction only for defects that construction itself executes.  I applied that rule carefully to witnesses 
all day and then made exactly the error it warns about when reasoning about GATES.  A gate that constructs a CPU is a 
rung-2 gate; these defects need a rung-3 one.\n\nTHE PRESCRIBED CLOSURE IS SMALLER THAN MY FRAMING SUGGESTED: wire the 
existing 19-row regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py into gate_ppc.sh, asserting its score AND its completion sentinel.  That 
closes the missing instruction-result check.  **It does NOT close all 64-bit PowerPC coverage**, and the seat says so 
explicitly rather than letting the round claim it does.\n\n*** DESIGNED 2026-09-10 
(ppcrldimimask/codex_repair_20260910.txt, GPT-6 Astra at max, ZERO cyber flags), AND IT FOUND A SECOND DEFECT AT THIS 
SITE THAT I HAD NOT SEEN.  MY 'ONE-TOKEN CAST' PREMISE IS REFUTED. ***\n\nThe width contamination is confirmed exactly 
as filed: for `1 <= SH <= 31' the erroneous expression's low word is `correct_rot32(L, SH) | ((H << SH) & 
0xffffffff)', and the existing low-word mask can select those bits.  `SH == 0' bypasses the shift expression and is 
clean.\n\n*** THE SECOND DEFECT: WHEN `MB > ME' THE ARCHITECTURAL MASK WRAPS ACROSS THE 64-BIT BOUNDARY AND COVERS THE 
ENTIRE HIGH HALF, so RA's high 32 bits must be REPLACED with the rotated word -- and this implementation preserves 
them unconditionally. ***  `ROTL32(x, y)' is defined as rotating `x || x', the low word DUPLICATED 
(`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:17143'), and the rlwimi RTL at `:29989' combines that with `MASK(MB+32, ME+32)'.  The 
wrapping mask visits `MB+32..63, 0..ME+32' -- which includes every architectural position `0..31'.\n\n    MB <= ME   
high 32 mask bits all ZERO  -> preserve old RA      (what the code does)\n    MB >  ME   high 32 mask bits all ONE   
-> replace with rot word (what the code never does)\n\n**So the repair is narrowing AND a high-word insertion**, and 
it must sit after the rotation and before the loop mutates `mb':\n\n        uint32_t tmp = 
(uint32_t)reg(ic->arg[0]);\n        MODE_uint_t ra = reg(ic->arg[1]);\n        ...\n        tmp = sh ? ((tmp << sh) | 
(tmp >> (32-sh))) : tmp;\n        /*  A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word.  */\n        if (mb > 
me)\n                ra = (ra & 0xffffffffULL) | ((uint64_t)tmp << 32);\n\nIn the 32-bit compilation the conversion 
back to `MODE_uint_t' discards that high half, so the existing behaviour there is unchanged.\n\nMUTANT, one character: 
`>' -> `<' in the new condition.  `rlwimi r4,r3,0,31,0' = **0x506407c0**, `r3 = 0xaaaaaaaa0000000f', `r4 = 
0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5a' gives correct `0x0000000f5a5a5a5b' and mutant `0x5a5a5a5a5a5a5a5b' -- the low-word insertion 
succeeds and the high-word replacement is skipped.\n\nTERMINATION PROVED rather than assumed: both fields are masked 
`& 31', `mb' is reset to 0 on reaching 32, positions are `(b+k) mod 32', and ME is processed after `((ME-b+32) % 32) + 
1' bodies -- between 1 and 32.  Every shift count `31-mb' is in range.\n\nROUND SCOPE: **TWO rounds, not one**, with 
[[ppcrldimimask]].  Same file and family, but one repairs operand lifetime and a circular inclusive traversal while 
the other repairs source width and a missing high-word insertion, and validating the second needs word-duplication and 
high-half expectations that lie outside the doubleword detector's contract.\n\nValidated offline over **655,360 
cases** (every SH/MB/ME at both widths x ten operand pairs).  **Not compiled** -- the seat had no native compiler and 
WSL returned `E_ACCESSDENIED'.\n\n**BLOCKER PARTLY CLEARED 2026-09-10:** [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s `want_rldimi' is 
corrected -- it was returning an all-zero mask for **2016 of 4096** (MB,SH) pairs, and the manual's own `:6882' (*\"It 
is not possible to specify an all-zero mask\"*) makes that a flat contradiction rather than a disputed reading.  
`mask32' is deliberately left alone pending [[ppcrlwhighhalf]].  A wrapping-encoding row measured RED on the committed 
build is still owed before either repair is scored.\n\n**THE HIGH-HALF INSERTION HALF IS CONFIRMED OWED, 2026-09-10.** 
 It was filed as depending on the [[ppcrlwhighhalf]] dispute; that dispute is now RESOLVED by PowerPC Book I v2.01, 
which states the wrapping MASK and makes no upper-half clearing claim.  So this round needs BOTH halves -- the source 
narrowing AND the `if (mb > me)` high-word insertion -- and the narrowing alone is not the instruction's 
repair.\n\n**THE ORACLE BLOCKER IS CLEARED, 2026-09-11.**  [[ppcwitnessmaskoracle]]'s two corrected helpers were 
verified against PowerPC Book I -- endpoints, wrap direction, the `+32` offsets, the `& 63`, the all-zero-mask 
assertion's normative standing, and both partition counts.  **PASS.**  A measuring seat can now score this round 
without the oracle being the thing that is wrong.\n\n*** CHECKED FOR THE INERT-REPAIR DEFECT THAT KILLED THE SIBLING 
DESIGN, 2026-09-11: THIS ONE IS SAFE, AND THE REASON IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN LUCKY. ***\n\n`ppcrlwhighhalf`'s 
adopted repair turned out INERT because the DECODER mutates `mb` to equal `me` before the handler selection that tests 
`mb > me`.  The same question had to be asked here, since the proposed repair uses the identical guard.\n\n**IT IS 
FINE, and the difference is WHERE `mb` COMES FROM:**\n\n        X(rlwimi):  int mb = (iword >> 6) & 31;        <-- 
decoded FRESH, in the handler\n                    tmp = sh ? (rotate) : tmp;\n                    if (mb > me) ...    
               <-- the proposed insertion, HERE\n                    for (;;) { ... mb ++; ... }        <-- mutation 
happens AFTER\n\n**`X(rlwimi)` does not consume a decoder-precomputed mask at all -- it decodes `mb` and `me` from 
`ic->arg[2]` itself -- so it never inherits the mutated value.**  `rlwinm` and `rlwnm` DO consume a precomputed mask, 
and their handler selection happens in the decoder, downstream of the loop that walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*So the two 
rounds are not two instances of one hazard: one repair sits upstream of its own mutation and the other sat downstream 
of someone else's.  Recording the reason rather than only the verdict, because a future round asking \"is my guard 
inert?\" needs the discriminator -- **does this code decode the value it tests, or receive it?** -- and not a list of 
which handlers happened to be safe.*\n\n*** MEASURED AND REPAIRED 2026-09-11 BY agy ON **GEMINI 3.8 FLASH (HIGH)** -- 
the first round on the 3.8 seat, switched and liveness-verified the same hour. ***\n\n**WITNESS IS TEST-FIRST AND THE 
ORDER IS PROVEN BY MTIME, not asserted:** `witness_committed.out` 00:46, `candidate_fix.diff` 00:47, `witness_fix.out` 
00:48.\n\n    committed build   14/28   -- 93 census cells differing from the architectural result\n    repaired build 
   28/28   -- 0 census cells differing, no failing rows\n\n**THE DIFF IS THE DESIGNED TWO-PART REPAIR, BOTH HALVES:** 
`uint32_t tmp = (uint32_t)reg(...)` splitting the declaration so `ra` keeps `MODE_uint_t`, and the `if (mb > me)` 
high-word insertion placed AFTER the rotate and BEFORE the loop -- which is the placement that keeps it from being 
inert, since the loop walks `mb` to `me`.\n\n*** AND IT FOLDED IN THE AXIS I HAD NOT ENUMERATED, WITHOUT BEING TOLD 
TO. ***  The pre-fix failing set includes **`alias nowrap`, `alias wrap`, `alias both`** -- REGISTER ALIASING, which a 
review named to me yesterday as an axis my four (mb, me, shift, compilation width) did not cover, and which I passed 
to this seat only as an open question.  **It rowed it.**  So `RA == RS` is measurably affected pre-fix and correct 
post-fix.\n\nThe rest of the pre-fix failing set spans both defects and both mask classes, which is what the brief 
demanded: three non-wrapping rows, four wrapping rows, `wrap fullword`, the two preservation rows, and the 
census.\n\n**STILL RUNNING when the print budget expired: the kill table** (`killtable_20260911/`).  Same orphan shape 
as this seat's earlier round today -- *\"root agent idle; waiting for 1 background task(s)\"* -- but the brief told it 
to write its report to disk as it goes, so the artifacts are accumulating rather than being held for a final flush.  
**A 120-minute print budget was not enough either; the lesson is to collect from the artifacts directory, not from the 
seat's stdout.**\n\n*** THE KILL TABLE IS COMPLETE AND STRONG, AND ITS `NULL_MEDIUM` LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL.  
Found by opening the mutator rather than reading the leg name. ***\n\n    BASE          28/28        M_ONLY_HIGH   
19/28\n    M_REV_COND    13/28        M_POST_LOOP   19/28\n    M_ONLY_CAST   19/28        M_ORIG        14/28   (= 
HEAD, identical failing set)\n    HEAD          14/28        RESTORED      28/28\n    NULL_MEDIUM   28/28        <-- 
SEE BELOW\n\nClearance: **Part 1 (cmp vs SEC) CLEAN, Part 2 (binary -nt source) FRESH, sentinel cleared.**\n\n**THREE 
LEGS EARN THEIR PLACE AND TWO OF THEM ANSWER QUESTIONS I ASKED EXPLICITLY:**\n  * **`M_ONLY_CAST` 19/28 and 
`M_ONLY_HIGH` 19/28** -- each half of the repair ALONE is killed, so **neither half suffices** and the two-part claim 
is measured rather than asserted.\n  * *** `M_POST_LOOP` 19/28 -- the INERT-PLACEMENT mutant. *** Moving the 
conditional after the loop is KILLED, which is the proof-by-measurement I asked for that the placement is 
load-bearing.  **That is the exact defect that made the sibling `rlwinm` design inert, and here it is a dead mutant 
instead of a shipped mistake.**\n\n*** BUT `NULL_MEDIUM` IS NOT A NULL MEDIUM. ***  The mutator reads `if leg in 
(\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"): replacement = FIXED_BLOCK` -- **so the leg applies the SAME fixed block as 
BASE and is BASE under another name.**  It scores 28/28 because it is identical, not because the detector survives a 
degenerate input.\n\n**VACUITY FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS THEREFORE UNDISCHARGED**: the detector must FAIL on a stale copy or 
a comment-only file, proving it carries identity rows and is actually testing the binary it claims.  A leg that cannot 
fail proves nothing and reads in the table as though it did -- *the check-that-cannot-fail shape, inside a floor 
clause whose whole purpose is to catch that shape.*\n\n**OWED BEFORE THIS SHIPS:** a real null medium -- run the 
frozen probe against a STALE binary, or against a source whose only change is a comment -- and show it RED.  Cheap, 
one leg, and it is the difference between claiming clause (c) and discharging it.\n\n*** 2026-09-11: THE KILL TABLE'S 
NULL-MEDIUM LEG IS A CHECK THAT CANNOT FAIL, SO VACUITY-FLOOR CLAUSE (c) IS UNDISCHARGED.  L2 -- repaired in-round. 
***\n\n`killtable_20260911/mutate_rlwimi.py:179` reads\n\n    if leg in (\"BASE\", \"NULL_MEDIUM\", \"RESTORED\"):\n   
     replacement = FIXED_BLOCK\n\nso the NULL_MEDIUM leg COMPILES BYTE-IDENTICAL CODE TO BASE.  Its 28/28 in 
`SCORES.txt` is not a result: all three legs are the same binary, and the leg would report 28/28 whether or not the 
detector could detect anything.\n\nClause (c) requires the detector to FAIL on a medium carrying none of the behaviour 
under test.  A leg that recompiles the fix is a third copy of the base leg wearing the clause's name.\n\n**This is the 
check-that-cannot-fail family arriving INSIDE the floor clause whose whole purpose is to catch vacuity** -- which is 
worth recording as its own shape.  The carrier's existing instances are a `say` in a shell script and a sentence in a 
seat table; this one is a kill-table leg, i.e. it sits in the measurement apparatus and renders as a scored row.  A 
reader of `SCORES.txt` sees four green identity-ish legs and reads three independent confirmations where there is 
one.\n\nNOTHING ELSE IN THE ROUND IS AFFECTED: the six real mutants each scored a genuine kill (M_REV_COND 13/28, 
M_ONLY_CAST 19/28, M_ONLY_HIGH 19/28, M_POST_LOOP 19/28, M_ORIG 14/28, HEAD 14/28), and M_POST_LOOP at 19/28 
independently proves the conditional's PLACEMENT is load-bearing.  The fix is not in question; the FLOOR CLAIM 
is.\n\nIN FLIGHT: agy 3.8 is building the real leg -- an identity `X(rlwimi)` body, in a scratch clone, no shared 
build tree touched -- where every row that genuinely observes rlwimi must RED.  Any row still green under an identity 
body is vacuous by construction and is the most valuable thing that run can return.  Brief predicts the `census` 
bookkeeping rows may legitimately survive; that is to be DISCLOSED with its reason, not waived.\n\nSTILL OWED after 
that: the full panel pass 2 on the diff.  Held rather than fired because a measuring seat's kill table was in flight 
and panel seats are CPU-heavy -- a deliberate hold, neither a seat failure nor agreement.\n\n*** CORRECTION 
2026-09-11: **the existing `regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py` has 24 ROWS, NOT 19.**  THE \"19-ROW\" FIGURE IS WRONG and it 
appears verbatim in this row and in its sibling. ***\n\nMeasured three ways: `git show 
e777ff0:regress/ppc_rldicl_probe.py | grep -c '^row('` = **24**; HEAD = 24; worktree = 24; and `git log --follow` 
shows no change since `e777ff0`.  `ppcwitnessmaskoracle` calls the same instrument a **\"24-row detector\"**, so the 
ledger contradicted itself.\n\n**19 is a SCORE, lifted from different artifacts** -- 
`regress/artifacts/ppcrldiclzero/witness_committed.out` reads `SCORE 19/27`, and the superseded 
`pass2_20260910/opus_probe_BASE.raw` reads `SCORE 19/19`.  *A score transcribed into a row-count column is the 
frozen-number class in a new coat, and this one is load-bearing: it is the prescribed gate-wiring closure, so a future 
round wiring \"19 rows\" into a gate would pin a denominator five short and the check would pass while testing less 
than it claims.*\n\n*** 2026-09-11 PASS 2 (codex GPT-6 Astra at max).  **Both algorithms VERIFIED; THE PROBE COVERS 
NEITHER FIX, AND THESE ARE TWO COMMITS NOT ONE.** ***\n\n*VERDICT: Both local algorithms check out; mode integration 
is unverified, and the probe covers neither fix.*\n\n**THE ARITHMETIC IS CONFIRMED, and by exhaustion rather than by 
argument:** independent in-memory models found **no mismatches across all 4,096 `rldimi` mask combinations and all 
1,024 `rlwimi` mask pairs**, plus operand checks covering every word-rotate encoding in BOTH destination widths.  
*Stated honestly as arithmetic checks, not compiled-C or emulator runs.*\n\n**`rldimi`: `(64 - sh) & 63` IS CORRECT.** 
 Book I's RTL is `m <- MASK(b, !n)` with `n` six bits, so `!n` is `63-SH` and the loop must stop once `m` has advanced 
past it.  `:4315` names that endpoint in prose.  *The pre-fix `63 - sh` read `sh` AFTER `while (sh-- != 0)` had left 
it at -1.*\n\n=== *** THE FINDING THAT CHANGES THE ROUND: NO DETECTOR COVERS EITHER FIX *** ===\n> *'There is no 
shared functioning detector here -- the permitted probe detects NEITHER repair.'*\n\n`ppc_rldicl_probe.py`'s two 
expected-value helpers exist but implement DISTINCT instruction formulas.  **So both fixes would ship with nothing 
that fails on the pre-fix build** -- vacuity floor clause (a) unmet, for both.  **Each commit owes 
instruction-specific observations that kill its own effect-nullifying mutant, including a SEPARATE WIDTH-SENSITIVE 
observation for `rlwimi`.**\n\n*** AND THEY ARE TWO COMMITS, NOT ONE.  The scope rule's join needs SAME SEMANTIC CLASS 
**AND ONE FIX DESIGN**; the class matches and the designs do not: *** `rldimi` preserves the shift amount and builds a 
correct inclusive circular mask; `rlwimi` narrows the rotation SOURCE and implements a duplicated high-word insertion. 
 *Even granting that the existing helpers satisfy 'no new oracle', the one-fix-design half fails.*\n\n=== THE 32-BIT 
MODE QUESTION: MY WORRY WAS HALF RIGHT, AND THE HALF THAT SURVIVES IS THE SHARPER ONE ===\nThe truncation is **sound 
as a representation argument, not an accident**: assignment to a 32-bit `MODE_uint_t` discards the upper bits by 
defined unsigned-conversion semantics, the shift itself happens at 64-bit width, and the loop then performs the 
low-word insertion.\n\n> *** BUT Book I S1.2 (`:342`): both execution modes affect ALL 64 BITS when an instruction 
sets a 64-bit register, and the register result is MODE-INDEPENDENT; `:1989` describes every GPR as 64 bits.  **So a 
64-bit processor running in 32-bit MODE still owes the wrapping high-word write.** ***\n\n**The permitted files do not 
define `MODE_uint_t` or `reg`, or establish which implementation selects each compiled variant, so the seat declined 
to certify it** -- *'If a 64-bit processor's 32-bit execution mode reaches a variant that loses the architectural high 
word, that remains wrong.'*  **Filed rather than guessed.**\n\n**L4 CORRECTION APPLIED IN PLACE:** the one-line 
comment *'A wrapping MASK(MB+32, ME+32) includes the high word'* invited the reading that architectural 32-bit mode 
removes half the register.  Replaced with the seat's more accurate wording plus the open question and its Book I 
citations.  *This file's idiom is `MODE32` with `#else` (see `extsb`/`extsh`) and carries no `MODE64` conditional, so 
a future guard would be `#ifndef MODE32` -- recorded so the next round does not invent the opposite 
convention.*\n\n**STATUS: arithmetic green, DETECTOR OWED for both, and the round splits in two.**  Landing is held by 
owner ruling until the full panel reports.",
