     "title": "cpu_ppc.c update_cr0(): the comparison width is chosen from the CPU MODEL width (cpu->cd.ppc.bits) 
rather than from the current operating mode, so a 64-bit CPU running with SF=0 records 64-bit condition bits",
>    "note": "FILED 2026-09-10 from the [[ppcrldimimask]] design review, which raised it as a limitation on its own 
repairs rather than as the thing it was asked about.  **Verified locally by opening the function.**\n\n`update_cr0()' 
branches on `if (cpu->cd.ppc.bits == 64)' and compares `(int64_t)value', else `(int32_t)value'.  `cpu->cd.ppc.bits' is 
a property of the configured CPU MODEL and does not change while the guest runs.  The manual requires the comparison 
to follow the current MODE (`ppc_pem_32and64_2005.txt:6357'), which on a 64-bit implementation is selected by the MSR 
SF bit and CAN change at run time.\n\nSo on a 64-bit CPU executing in 32-bit mode, a record-form result whose low word 
is negative but whose full 64-bit value is positive records GT where the architecture requires LT.\n\n**SCOPE IS THE 
WHOLE RECORD FORM, NOT ONE INSTRUCTION**: every `_dot' handler in the tree routes through this one function, which is 
why it is filed on its own rather than inside either PowerPC rotate row.  The seat's own statement of the consequence 
is the honest bound to carry forward: *those repairs do not establish correct record-form behaviour on a 64-bit CPU 
operating with SF=0.*\n\nLOW because reachability is unestablished in the sharpest way: it needs a 64-bit PowerPC CPU 
running with SF clear, and this tree has no gate that executes ANY 64-bit PowerPC instruction and checks the result -- 
see [[wiringcliff]].  A round here owes the mode plumbing first, and should check whether `cpu->cd.ppc.bits' is 
consulted this way anywhere else.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2026-09-11: **\"EVERY `_dot` HANDLER IN THE TREE ROUTES THROUGH 
THIS ONE FUNCTION\" IS FALSE**, and that sentence is this row's stated warrant for filing separately (\"SCOPE IS THE 
WHOLE RECORD FORM\"). ***\n\n`cpu_ppc_instr.c:205-206`:\n\n    #define FDOT(n) X(n ## _dot) { CHECK_FOR_FPU_EXCEPTION; 
\\\n            instr(n)(cpu,ic); update_cr1(cpu); }\n\n`grep -c '^FDOT('` = **25 `_dot` handlers routing through 
`update_cr1()`**, not `update_cr0()`.  The real population is the 33 `DOT0`/`DOT1`/`DOT2` uses plus 4 explicit 
`update_cr0` call sites.\n\n**The CODE is right** -- floating-point record forms SHOULD set CR1 -- and the defect 
itself is unaffected.  What is wrong is the SCOPE sentence, and it is wrong in the expensive direction: *a round 
scoping its work from that sentence would sweep in 25 handlers the defect cannot touch.*",
