cheaper witness, no fake-green hazard.  GATE 14's 358 checks never feed the encoding.  *** REWRITE, NOT PATCH.  THE 
16-ROW SPEC PLUS EIGHT AMENDMENTS DOES NOT CLOSE, AND THE SEAT THAT WROTE THE ORIGINAL SPEC RULED AGAINST ITS OWN WORK 
WHEN ASKED TO BE HARDER ON IT. ***  I asked plainly whether eight amendments on a 16-row spec meant the spec should be 
rewritten rather than patched, and named the `#392` shape as the thing I feared.  Answer: yes.  'A builder applying 
them in order will get 1+6 wrong and will either treat `save 2s` as a duplicate of `kill 15` or REPLACE `kill 15` with 
it, losing the `== 0x14` pin.'  *** AND THE TALLY ITSELF WAS A FROZEN NUMBER: it is 14/17 -> 17/17 over THIRTEEN 
sessions, not 14/16 -> 16/16 over twelve.  The original 12 was already false -- 16 scored minus `enc ok` minus the 
identity row minus one for live/pc/armed sharing S1 is 13, and the spec wrote 12. ***  THE VEHICLE STANDS and is 
unchanged: `-V -A -E testarm -M 64`, `0xE321F014`, member 3 at BOTH `default:` arms, one fresh process per scored row 
that executes a guest instruction, mid-stream dump.  The ROW LIST, the ORACLES and the SESSION ARITHMETIC are 
replaced. **ANSWER TO MY INTERACTION QUESTION 1+6, AND IT REFUTES MY FRAMING.**  I asked whether, once amendment 1 
makes EOF-alone the kill trigger, the `enc ok` / `no cbit` / dump-before-MSR machinery becomes THE ONLY THING between 
the witness and a false green.  The answer is that those three are **NECESSARY AND NOT SUFFICIENT** -- each has a 
smallest defeat: dump-DEST proves MRS ran, not that the MSR word was planted or stepped; `no cbit` is greened by a 
decoder that never writes the mode field AT ALL, which greens every kill row too; and `enc ok` walking a constructor 
instead of the planted literal is the BFX incident repeating.  What actually closes false-green, all four required: 
(1) **POST want is `m=13 sp once`, not `m=13`** -- member 3 prints `(once)`, and encoding-nop, skipped-step and a 
silent member-2 all print nothing, so the latch must be IN the got string or its absence is a comment rather than a 
FAIL; (2) **poison `DEST+4` with 0xdeadbeef** and dump DEST+4, never DEST+0, so a never-fired post-MSR store reds; (3) 
**one `WORDS` dict**, with every `put w` drawn from it and a per-session plant-dump of the MSR slot -- this is the 
load-bearing anti-false-green that amendment 6 hoped `enc ok` was; (4) **named-red tally** -- HEAD must be EXACTLY the 
three named reds, because 17/17 on HEAD is not success. **ANSWER TO INTERACTION 3+5, AND AMENDMENT 3 IS HALF-RIGHT AND 
MISREADS ITS OWN CASE.**  The original two reds were `kill 14` and `kill 15` -- TWO LOAD HOLES catching the `== 0x14` 
special case -- **not** Load vs Save, which is what amendment 3 assumed.  So: SCORE `save 2s`, KEEP `kill 15`, do NOT 
replace one with the other.  The underlying worry stands: without a scored two-write row a Load-only skip-memcpy POSTs 
clean and still dies on the write OUT of reserved mode (16/17, `save 2s` red on the second MSR -- that is the point of 
the row). **AMENDMENT 5 IS TRUE OF THE ENCODING AND INERT FOR THIS PROBE, VERIFIED LOCALLY BY ME AND NOT TAKEN FROM 
THE SEAT:** `#0x14` does clear I and F, and testarm does hang cons/ether/rtc off `irqc` -- but ARM IRQ dispatch sits 
inside `if (!single_step) {` at `cpu_dyntrans.c:206` with the ARM arm at `:208-209`, and `-V` sets single_step; 
**`fiq_asserted` has ZERO occurrences across all of `src/`** and `ARM_FLAG_F` zero in `cpu_dyntrans.c`, so there is no 
ARM FIQ dispatch to enable; and debugger interaction `timer_stop()`s so `step N` runs no host-timer callback.  **The 
pin is STAY ON `step`, NEVER `continue`** -- a `continue` after a LANDED `#0x14` can take `arm_exception` and run Save 
on the reserved current mode, which is a free-run hazard and not a step hazard.  DO NOT change the filed stimulus to 
`#0xD4`. **BOTH KILL ARMS CONFIRMED AT HEAD BY READING:** `cpu_arm.c:559-561` `arm_save_register_bank: unimplemented 
mode %i` + `exit(1)`, and `:606-608` the same for `arm_load_register_bank`.  Two arms, two kills -- which is exactly 
why member 3 is owed at both and why `save 2s` is independent rather than a duplicate. **DROPPED FROM THE ORIGINAL 
SPEC:** EOF-AND-text as the kill trigger; the 12 sessions; 'two reds because two default: arms'; POST `0xd3` on `kill 
14` (member 3 refuses the MODE FIELD, so I|F stay clear at `0x13` -- record I|F, do not want them); already-USR32 as 
the fake-green mechanism; copy-flags-`session()`; copy-flags-`printed()` (arm_flags_probe.py has none).  Amendment 8 
belongs on the ALPHA spec, not this one. **CLAUSE (e), DISCLOSED NOT ROWED:** register-form MSR (`X(msr)` copies rm 
into arg[0] and calls msr_imm); the 26-bit holes; the other 32-bit holes 0x16/18/19/1a/1c/1d/1e (`kill 14` + `kill 15` 
catch a ONE-VALUE special case, not a two-value one); SPSR-then-`MOVS pc`; free-run IRQ after a landed reserved write, 
pinned by stay-on-step. **BUILD FROM THE ARTIFACT, NOT FROM THIS NOTE** -- the complete 17-row table, the 13-session 
schedule, the CONTROL=DEAD set, the plant layouts and the tally live verbatim at 
`regress/artifacts/armmsrmodekill/WITNESSSPEC_REWRITE_grok_20260917.txt`.  Transcribing a row table into a ledger note 
is the frozen-number class waiting to happen. **ONE BUILD REQUIREMENT I FOUND MYSELF AND THE SPEC DOES NOT STATE:** 
the launch line uses `regress/arm_bfx_stub.bin`, which is on the NEVER-STAGE list and is UNTRACKED.  That is safe, and 
the reason matters -- **all 11 top-level committed probes referencing such a stub CREATE IT IF ABSENT** (e.g. 
`alpha_harness_probe.py:169-173`).  `arm_msrmode_probe.py` MUST carry the same self-create block, or it will be the 
one committed probe that cannot run on a fresh clone.  *** THE PROBE WAS ATTACKED BEFORE IT WAS EVER RUN, AND IT HAD 
THREE INDEPENDENT DEFECTS. Verdict: 'It runs.  It does not score.' ***  (1) **LINE LABELS ARE 16-BYTE ALIGNED** -- 
`debugger_cmds.c:709` is `addr = addr_start & ~0xf`, so `dump 0x9004` labels the line `0x00009000` AND BLANK-PADS the 
leading word groups, shifting every group index.  (2) **THE DUMP RENDERS LITTLE-ENDIAN BYTES, NOT A WORD** -- 0x2a 
prints as `2a000000`.  (3) **`save 2s` READ A SLOT NOTHING WROTE**: its plant put SP at DEST+16 via `str_sp_10` while 
`classify()` read DEST+8, so ` sp` could NEVER appear and **the row was ALWAYS RED, pre-fix and post-fix alike** -- 
not a witness of Save at all. *** THE REMEDIES FOR (1) AND (2) ALREADY EXISTED IN THIS TREE AND I DID NOT USE THEM: 
`arm_bdt_probe.py:123-149` records the alignment measurement from 2026-09-11 in a seven-line comment, and 
`arm_flags_probe.py:191-193` carries `sw()` for the byte order. Knowledge present, unconsulted at the moment of use -- 
the carrier's own recurring finding.  The repair COPIES both rather than inventing. *** **AND THE FAILURE MODE WOULD 
NOT HAVE BEEN A FALSE GREEN, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING.**  `live` wants 0x2a and would have got 0x2a000000; 
`armed` would have given `?/?`.  Both sit in the CONTROL=DEAD set, so the probe would have printed 
`ARMMSRMODE_CONTROL=DEAD`, `RESULT=0/0` and 'SETUP FAILED -- NOT no defect'.  **The vehicle-kill design worked exactly 
as intended: it would have REFUSED TO SCORE rather than lying.**  That is the difference between a wasted run and a 
wrong record, and it is why the CONTROL=DEAD set is not decoration. *A self-check while repairing it showed why 
`arm_flags_probe.py` warns as it does: `deadbeef` read RAW yields 0xdeadbeef, so the POISON comparison would have 
MATCHED under the broken parser while every real value was wrong -- the sentinel's own byte pattern hiding the 
defect.* **ONE CORRECTION RUNNING THE OTHER WAY, against the SPEC rather than the probe:** `print pc` wanting CODE+8 
after `step 2` is RIGHT; the spec table's CODE+4 is the wrong half, and a builder must not 'fix' the probe down to it. 
 REPAIRED, AST clean, zero raw `dump` commands left.  **STILL NOT RUN** -- the adjudicated ship order puts the commit 
first, then this.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the 
distinction that settles it is worth more than the verdict: **the gap on ip30 is 'we do not run that OS here'; the gap 
on armmsr is 'that OS would not do this.'  Only the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the 
documented interrupt path of the guest the machine exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to 
program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / 
`arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr & 0x1f`: **7 arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 
0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 
0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c 'case ARM_MODE_USR26'` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are 
SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; 
**0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I A2.5.7 says *'If any other value is programmed into the 
mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE'*, and there are NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on 
any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr 
cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore 
is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#<legal> ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  **Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the 
c-byte in USR32 (`mask &= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that 
could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and `MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no 
mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a correct kernel's SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception 
entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux's `valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not 
USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR 
`armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 
encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  
**`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep 
runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**",
