>    "state_note": "[2026-10-18, #566] the detector runs in gate 14 (41 checks).  The shipped 33-row instrument's 
matched pair: PRE-FIX 10/33 (23 rows red, every abort site among them) -> POST-FIX 33/33, one instrument, HEAD's 
sources in a scratch clone (regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/gatewire_20261018/legs/).  Still owed before close 
(target: before the 2026-10-25 battery): the witness field still leads with the 21-row pair; clause (b) -- abort() 
re-inserted at each fixed site of the FIXED file, re-run at 33 rows -- is held by no kill table; and a ruling on 
whether V7_nocounter and V8_len4 need a carrier that survives the close (Grok 4.7: no).  A load stall can read green 
in gate 14: ip30stallgreen.  ||  *** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES 
SIGABRT. AN `exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, 
all verified by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; 
(3) :145 `case 0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) 
registered at :387, the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is 
ADVERTISED at `machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, \"IP30\", 30, \"ip30\", \"octane\", NULL)`. 
0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE 
ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d->isr = idata;` 
FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a 
guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION 
AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set 
ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d->isr &= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  
`case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d->isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a 
detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  
`case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d->imask0 & 
(1<<50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code 
path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION 
SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting 
beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), 
which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the 
interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed 
device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  
No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind 
spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is 
on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO 
in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and 
`vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on 
unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. 
NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` 
sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five 
families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than 
construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 
9's blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site 
was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (\"No abort() is guest-reachable is 
FALSE\"), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month.  *** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT 
AND MEASURED 2026-09-17.  THE DEFECT IS NO LONGER CONFIRMED-BY-READING; IT IS MEASURED. ***  `IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK`, 
`IP30ABORT_RESULT=6/9` on the committed build, with the three kill rows RED -- which IS the reproduction, since the 
house polarity is WANT = POST-FIX CORRECT.  Measured, on unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30' with no `device_add' introduced 
to reach the site: **a guest LOAD at 0x0ff10020 kills the host** (`kill 20 rd' DEAD); so does a load at 0x10028; so 
does a WRITE at 0x10030 -- while a READ at 0x10030 is SERVICED (`acc 30 rd' alive).  **The three arms are provably NOT 
uniform**, which was a reading and is now a measurement.  The raw disposition was captured separately: `KILLED BY 
SIGNAL 6 (SIGABRT) after 0.6 s`, and the tree installs no SIGABRT handler. *** AND THE FIRST RUN OF THAT PROBE 
REPORTED `alive' FOR ALL THREE KILL ROWS -- A FALSE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST DEATH -- FOR A REASON WORTH CARRYING INTO 
EVERY EOF-ORACLE PROBE IN THIS TREE. ***  `rd()` RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF instead of waiting out its timeout, so the 
`spin` that exists to give a dying child time to die is SKIPPED EXACTLY WHEN THE CHILD DIES.  A single 
`waitpid(WNOHANG)` then samples ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is still writing a 6 MB core, reports 'still running', 
and the kill row goes GREEN.  **The fix is to POLL with a deadline, never to sample once** -- and 
`arm_msrmode_probe.py`, whose every kill row is an EOF oracle, MUST carry the polling form or its witness will certify 
the MSR defect fixed while it is live. *A second apparatus error in the same run, caught by the same disagreement: my 
`devsig` and `acc 18` oracles wanted truncated values, but `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS on this 64-bit R10000, so a CORRECT 
device reddened.  The apparatus was wrong, not the model.* ## FIX DESIGN, ADJUDICATED -- **MEMBER 3 + MEMBER 4, AND 
THE writeflag TEST IN THE SAME DIFF.** *** MY MEMBER-7 PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY A REPO FACT, NOT BY ARGUMENT. ***  I 
filed that member 7 IMPLEMENT was the strong candidate because every site carries its intended model beside it as a 
commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)`.  **THAT API NO LONGER EXISTS.**  Verified locally: `cpu_interrupt(` occurs FIVE 
times in the entire tree -- one warning comment in `dev_lca.c` and the four commented-out calls here -- with no 
definition and no declaration; the live API is `interrupt_assert` through `struct interrupt` (`cpu_mips.c:1884`).  
**Uncommenting does not compile.**  The tell was right (a stubbed path with no predicate); the inference that it was 
cheap was wrong. Further, `machine_sgi.c:415` for case 30 is literally `// TODO: Interrupts!` -- no INTERRUPT_CONNECT, 
no HEART lines -- and the serial is already routed AROUND HEART onto `cpu.2`.  The fossil literals use a DELETED 
numbering scheme (`8 + x`, so the tick's `cpu_interrupt(cpu, 8+1+50)` is 59), so translating them onto 
`INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu.2)` would be a WRONG MODEL, not a restore.  A cheap HEART sharing `cpu.2` with the 16550 breaks 
on deassert (assert ORs, deassert ANDs off), which is exactly why CRIME exists.  **`#516` refused member 7 on 
`lca_interrupt_assert` after naming the same tell -- the precedent is in-tree.** **MEMBER 7 IS THE FOLLOW-UP ROW** 
(implement HEART the way `dev_sgi_ip32.c` implements CRIME: INTERRUPT_CONNECT to cpu.2, named child lines, a reassert 
function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device `return 
0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, comments, 
and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for holes.  
*Note the brief's own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a 
description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND 
IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction 
check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than 
today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the 
twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not 
break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU 
DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` 
abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is 
minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does 
not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not 
merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not 'maybe an accept row' -- for the tick 
it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host 
SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must 
be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA's 
assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` 
for unknown offsets; **this file's OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file's outliers, not the 
house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe 
covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 
cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run 
distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed.  ## *** PANEL 
PASS 1 -- FOUR CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM TO WRONG RECORDS I WROTE THIS SESSION. *** (7 of 8 scriptable transports; 
codex still running at write time; Fable held for last.) **(A) DISPOSITION 1 IS WRONG AS RECORDED, and filing the 
follow-up row on it would ship a false premise.**  I recorded *'Member 7 IMPLEMENT is NOT available.'*  **The correct 
statement is AVAILABLE-BUT-UNWARRANTED.**  What does not exist is `cpu_interrupt()`; the LIVE API -- 
`interrupt_assert`/`interrupt_deassert` through `struct interrupt` -- exists and is exactly what `dev_sgi_ip32.c` uses 
for CRIME.  So member 7 is not blocked by the absence of an API; it is declined because DELIVERY IS A SUBSYSTEM nobody 
has costed (no INTERRUPT_CONNECT for ip30, the 16550 already on `cpu.2`, and a deleted `8 + x` numbering).  *The 
distinction is not pedantic: 'not available' would make the filed follow-up row unbuildable by definition, when in 
fact it is ordinary unscheduled work.*  (glm, who noticed that my own finding names the live API two sentences after 
declaring the member unavailable.) **(B) DISPOSITION 4'S WORD 'SEPARATE' IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THE PACKET.**  grok 
and the kimi substitute reached this independently.  The missing `writeflag` test is **the store-arm of THIS 
de-fatalisation** -- the same three `case` labels, the same function, the same common tail -- so it is ONE DIFF either 
way: *if you keep a store, a load must not execute it; if you decline the store, the load is already safe.*  **Calling 
it 'separate' is what made my own Q4 (scope-creep?) askable at all**, so the framing manufactured the question.  It is 
genuinely separate only at `0x10030`, which is already writeflag-tested.  kimi's sharper form: at 0x10020/0x10028 the 
missing test **is the read-kill's entry condition**, not a second defect. **(C) MY Q2 WAS REFUTED BY MY OWN PACKET** 
-- independently by kimi and deepseek.  I asked whether keeping `d->isr = idata` is member 4 given that the store 
updates *'a register nothing reads for any purpose'*.  **The same packet states that `case 0x10030`'s READ returns 
`d->isr`.**  The register IS read, by a path the packet itself documents and the witness measures (`acc 30 rd` alive). 
 The question was malformed; the answer is that the store feeds a live read path, which is member 4 ACCEPT on its own 
terms and not member 6. **(D) TWO WARRANTS SHARPENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED.**  Disposition 3's warrant cited a PROM 
that 'already talks to 0x10018' -- but 0x10018 is a NEIGHBOUR, so that sentence is surplus to the claim it was 
supporting (grok).  Disposition 6 holds, and its in-tree warrant is better taken from CRIME's *'one bit per site, 
never shared'* than from LCA's two-functions case; the hazard is concrete -- **one shared bit lets a Set-ISR write 
