wrong enumeration does not close a chain -- this round supplied that counter-example itself at table 2. ***  TEN 
CELLS: {4 announce sites} x {announce fires, latch sets} + {2 collision directions}.  Six closed before table 4; 
tables 4 and 5 killed the remaining four (V1/V2 the two collision directions, V4/V5 the two remaining latch-sets, V6 
the tick latch-set).  TWO cells are DISCLOSED WITH A RULING and BOTH WERE MEASURED TO ESCAPE -- the counter 
(fix-independent, fable5) and the width axis at 0x10020 (outside the floor by the pcc2widthkills bound).  ZERO CELLS 
OUTSIDE THE ENUMERATION.  *** That is the fable5 fork's FIRST branch, so the chain CLOSES and the escape hatch -- drop 
the latch axis wholesale to disclosed debt -- REMAINS UNSPENT. ***  A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION THAT HELD, recorded 
before the result arrived: PRETABLE5 said V1 kills via `xbit rev' but did not say whether `xbit rev2' would ALSO red.  
I derived beforehand that it would not -- under V1 the ISR assignment clobbers nothing because TICK was never set, the 
tick then sets its own bit, and the second ISR access is correctly silent.  MEASURED: `xbit rev' ALONE.  *** So the 
two collision rows separate BY DIRECTION, which is stronger than either row firing on both: the signature identifies 
WHICH clobber, not merely that one happened. ***  V1 at 32/33 (was 31/32 with one row red in table 4) also discharges 
the #462 regression check: adding `xbit rev2' did not perturb it.  That claim was REASONED in PRETABLE5 and is now 
MEASURED.  CONTRACT UPGRADED: the width disclosure now reads MEASURED TO ESCAPE rather than 'not swept'.  *** A 
disclosure backed by a MEASUREMENT tells a reader which edit walks free; one backed by an OMISSION only tells them 
nobody looked. ***  The incidental finding from the mis-built mutant is kept in the contract too: this detector DOES 
catch a width narrowing at imask0, with a four-row signature -- coverage on an arm the round never enumerated.",
     "entries": [
      {
       "seat": "grok",
       "phase": "research",
       "date": "2026-09-17",
       "grade": "CONFIRMED",
       "grader": "grok 4.6 xhigh, fired on an open critical-bug hunt asking what guest-reachable host death or 
guest-state corruption an `exit(` grep CANNOT find, with an honesty clause that an empty result with its denominator 
is a real answer. EVERY load-bearing claim re-derived locally before filing; two facts were added by that 
re-derivation and one citation (machine_m88k.c) corrected.",
>      "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/EXITHUNT_grok_20260917.txt and its brief; 
src/devices/dev_sgi_ip30.c:44,78,99-105,123-150,387; src/machines/machine_sgi.c:417,744",
       "note": "*** ONE GUEST LOAD OR STORE AT 0x0ff10020 ON THE ADVERTISED `-E sgi -e ip30` RAISES SIGABRT. AN 
`exit(` SWEEP CANNOT SEE IT, BECAUSE `abort()` CONTAINS NO `exit(`. ***  FOUR abort() SITES IN ONE FILE, all verified 
by reading dev_sgi_ip30.c at HEAD:  (1) :127 `case 0x10020` Set ISR; (2) :135 `case 0x10028` Clear ISR; (3) :145 `case 
0x10030` write arm; (4) :78 the DEVICE_TICK. REACHABILITY: `DEV_SGI_IP30_LENGTH` is 0x80000 (:44) registered at :387, 
the device is added at `machine_sgi.c:417` as `sgi_ip30 addr=0x0ff00000`, and the subtype is ADVERTISED at 
`machine_sgi.c:744` -- `machine_entry_add_subtype(me, \"IP30\", 30, \"ip30\", \"octane\", NULL)`. 
0x10020/0x10028/0x10030 all fall inside the window. *** TWO FACTS THE HUNTING SEAT DID NOT STATE, FOUND BY READING THE 
ARM, AND BOTH RAISE THE SEVERITY: ***  (A) **`case 0x10020` HAS NO `writeflag` TEST AND EXECUTES `d->isr = idata;` 
FIRST.**  `idata` is initialised to 0 at :87 and assigned ONLY under `if (writeflag == MEM_WRITE)` at :89-90, so a 
guest **LOAD** at 0x0ff10020 ZEROES THE INTERRUPT STATUS REGISTER and *then* aborts.  That is GUEST-STATE CORRUPTION 
AND A HOST DEATH IN THE SAME ARM, and it means the defect is not write-only as a reader would assume from the `Set 
ISR` comment.  `case 0x10028` shares the missing test (its `d->isr &= ~idata` is a no-op at idata==0, then aborts).  
`case 0x10030` IS writeflag-tested and reads return `d->isr` correctly -- so the three arms are NOT uniform, and a 
detector must row them separately.  (B) **THE TICK ABORT IS ARMED BY A LEGAL WRITE TO AN IMPLEMENTED REGISTER.**  
`case 0x10000` (:99-105) implements imask0 and ACCEPTS the write; the tick at :74-78 then aborts whenever `d->imask0 & 
(1<<50)`.  So the guest makes an accepted, unremarkable register write and the host dies LATER, from a different code 
path, with no access in flight.  That is the de-escalation shape at its worst: the announcing access succeeds. OPTION 
SET: member **7 IMPLEMENT** is the strong candidate -- every one of the four sites has the intended model sitting 
beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)` call, so this is a stubbed path (no predicate refusing a value), 
which is exactly the tell member 7 was minted for. Member **3 DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE** is the fallback if the 
interrupt rewrite stays out of scope.  Member 5 FAULT is architecturally available here (MIPS dispatches on a failed 
device access) but is INERT as the code stands, because `abort()` fires before any `return 0`. GATE COVERAGE: NONE.  
No ip30 probe exists under `regress/`.  Gate 9 may CONSTRUCT the machine, but nothing feeds 0x10020 -- the same blind 
spot as `armmsrmodekill`. SEVERITY HIGH: `abort()` is strictly worse than `exit(1)` (SIGABRT plus a core dump), it is 
on an advertised subtype, and one of the four paths corrupts guest state before dying.  Honest limit: there is NO 
in-tree Octane boot image, so this ranks probe-HIGH and not boot-HIGH -- the same standing as `mapledmaaddr` and 
`vmedefaultkill`.  Reachability is CONFIRMED BY READING and is NOT YET MEASURED; the round owes a rung-3 probe on 
unmodified `-E sgi -e ip30` (`ld`/`sd` at 0x0ff10020), plus a separate row for the armed-tick path, before any edit. 
NO EXISTING ROW COVERS THIS.  Nine ledger rows mention ip30 or abort(): `exitsweep` is CLOSED and counts `exit(` 
sites, not `abort()`; `ctorabortclass` is HELD and is the CONSTRUCTION abort class (nine subtypes across five 
families, none of them ip30 -- ip30 constructs fine, which is why this one is guest-reachable rather than 
construction-time); `hpcabort` is CLOSED and is hpcmips construction; `asansweepblind` is DROPPED and is about gate 
9's blindness to construction aborts.  `exitunowned76` counts `exit(`.  `cpusexitsweep` is src/cpus/ only.  The site 
was NAMED ONCE, in the 2026-08-17 `exitsweep` correction in OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md (\"No abort() is guest-reachable is 
FALSE\"), and never given an id -- so it has been a known-but-unqueued exposure for a month."
      },
      {
       "seat": "opus5",
       "phase": "assess",
       "date": "2026-09-17",
       "grade": "CONFIRMED",
       "grader": "main loop, rung-3 witness BUILT AND RUN against the committed build/gxemul (itself freshly 
two-part-cleared the same hour).  Two apparatus errors of mine were caught inside the run and are recorded rather than 
quietly fixed.",
>      "ev": "regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/ip30_abort_probe.py and probe_HEAD_20260917.out; 
src/devices/dev_sgi_ip30.c:78,127,135,145",
       "note": "*** RUNG-3 WITNESS BUILT AND MEASURED 2026-09-17.  THE DEFECT IS NO LONGER CONFIRMED-BY-READING; IT IS 
MEASURED. ***  `IP30ABORT_CONTROL=OK`, `IP30ABORT_RESULT=6/9` on the committed build, with the three kill rows RED -- 
which IS the reproduction, since the house polarity is WANT = POST-FIX CORRECT.  Measured, on unmodified `-E sgi -e 
ip30' with no `device_add' introduced to reach the site: **a guest LOAD at 0x0ff10020 kills the host** (`kill 20 rd' 
DEAD); so does a load at 0x10028; so does a WRITE at 0x10030 -- while a READ at 0x10030 is SERVICED (`acc 30 rd' 
alive).  **The three arms are provably NOT uniform**, which was a reading and is now a measurement.  The raw 
disposition was captured separately: `KILLED BY SIGNAL 6 (SIGABRT) after 0.6 s`, and the tree installs no SIGABRT 
handler. *** AND THE FIRST RUN OF THAT PROBE REPORTED `alive' FOR ALL THREE KILL ROWS -- A FALSE GREEN ON A LIVE HOST 
DEATH -- FOR A REASON WORTH CARRYING INTO EVERY EOF-ORACLE PROBE IN THIS TREE. ***  `rd()` RETURNS IMMEDIATELY ON EOF 
instead of waiting out its timeout, so the `spin` that exists to give a dying child time to die is SKIPPED EXACTLY 
WHEN THE CHILD DIES.  A single `waitpid(WNOHANG)` then samples ~0 s after EOF while the kernel is still writing a 6 MB 
core, reports 'still running', and the kill row goes GREEN.  **The fix is to POLL with a deadline, never to sample 
once** -- and `arm_msrmode_probe.py`, whose every kill row is an EOF oracle, MUST carry the polling form or its 
witness will certify the MSR defect fixed while it is live. *A second apparatus error in the same run, caught by the 
same disagreement: my `devsig` and `acc 18` oracles wanted truncated values, but `lw` SIGN-EXTENDS on this 64-bit 
R10000, so a CORRECT device reddened.  The apparatus was wrong, not the model.* ## FIX DESIGN, ADJUDICATED -- **MEMBER 
3 + MEMBER 4, AND THE writeflag TEST IN THE SAME DIFF.** *** MY MEMBER-7 PREMISE WAS REFUTED BY A REPO FACT, NOT BY 
ARGUMENT. ***  I filed that member 7 IMPLEMENT was the strong candidate because every site carries its intended model 
beside it as a commented-out `cpu_interrupt(...)`.  **THAT API NO LONGER EXISTS.**  Verified locally: `cpu_interrupt(` 
occurs FIVE times in the entire tree -- one warning comment in `dev_lca.c` and the four commented-out calls here -- 
with no definition and no declaration; the live API is `interrupt_assert` through `struct interrupt` 
(`cpu_mips.c:1884`).  **Uncommenting does not compile.**  The tell was right (a stubbed path with no predicate); the 
inference that it was cheap was wrong. Further, `machine_sgi.c:415` for case 30 is literally `// TODO: Interrupts!` -- 
no INTERRUPT_CONNECT, no HEART lines -- and the serial is already routed AROUND HEART onto `cpu.2`.  The fossil 
literals use a DELETED numbering scheme (`8 + x`, so the tick's `cpu_interrupt(cpu, 8+1+50)` is 59), so translating 
them onto `INTERRUPT_ASSERT(cpu.2)` would be a WRONG MODEL, not a restore.  A cheap HEART sharing `cpu.2` with the 
16550 breaks on deassert (assert ORs, deassert ANDs off), which is exactly why CRIME exists.  **`#516` refused member 
7 on `lca_interrupt_assert` after naming the same tell -- the precedent is in-tree.** **MEMBER 7 IS THE FOLLOW-UP 
ROW** (implement HEART the way `dev_sgi_ip32.c` implements CRIME: INTERRUPT_CONNECT to cpu.2, named child lines, a 
reassert function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device 
`return 0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, 
comments, and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for 
holes.  *Note the brief's own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a 
description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND 
IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction 
check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than 
today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the 
twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not 
break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU 
DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` 
abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is 
minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does 
not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not 
merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not 'maybe an accept row' -- for the tick 
it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host 
SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must 
be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA's 
assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` 
for unknown offsets; **this file's OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file's outliers, not the 
house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe 
covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 
cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run 
distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed."
      },
