     "title": "dev_8259.c halts the emulated CPU on an unimplemented PIC command at the command port and then returns 
1, so the access succeeds while the strand stops -- invisible to both an exit( and an abort() sweep",
>    "state_note": "`dev_8259.c` turns an unimplemented PIC command into a GUEST-VISIBLE HALT: the command-port arm 
does `fatal(\"[ 8259: unimplemented command 0x%02x ]\")` and then `cpu->running = 0`, and **then still returns 1** -- 
so the access 'succeeds' while the strand stops.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:140`, `:231`, `:235` (`:173` is 
inside a comment and is NOT a site). **DISTINCT FROM A HOST DEATH, and the ranking matters:** `cpu->running = 0` is a 
FUNCTIONAL HALT -- the process lives, the emulated CPU stops.  That ranks BELOW a SIGABRT/exit(1) and ABOVE a silent 
wrong value, and it is invisible to BOTH an `exit(` sweep and an `abort()` sweep, which is why the class survived 
three censuses. REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED** -- the seat's claim is that `DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2 so the 
`default:` arm is dead, and the live door is OFFSET 0, the command port, taking any OCW that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b 
/ 0x0c / an EOI form -- i.e. `strb r0,[pic]` with r0 = 0 would be enough on CATS, where `bus_isa.c` installs it.  
**NONE of that is re-verified here and it is explicitly not claimed.**  A census job is auditing it; this row exists 
so the finding cannot be lost again while that runs. *** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was 
named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt' seat, which returned FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, 
`mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT 
THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so that file had never been queued at all.  
**That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found real defects sitting in**, and here the 
review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17 BY THE 
HALT-CLASS CENSUS, AND TWO OF THE THREE SITES I LISTED CANNOT RUN. ***  I filed `:140`, `:231`, `:235`.  
**`DEV_8259_LENGTH` is 2** (`dev_8259.c:47`, registered `:278`), and `memory_rw` only calls a handler for `paddr` in 
`[base, endaddr)` then subtracts the base -- so `relative_addr` is 0 or 1, both have `case` labels, and **the 
`default:` arm at `:228-236` is DEAD BY WINDOW SIZE.**  **THE LIVE SITE IS `:140` ALONE.**  *** AND MY 'the access 
still returns 1, so it SUCCEEDS' CLAIM IS ALSO WRONG. ***  `memory_rw.c` SNAPSHOTS `cpu->running` around the handler 
and, if it dropped, returns `MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED` **before** it looks at `res` -- so the access does NOT report 
success.  What is true, and is the part worth keeping, is that there is **no `MEM_ARM` case** in that block, so ARM 
gets no device-fail abort: it is a HALT, not a FAULT, and member 5 is not wired for ARM device failures. **THE DOOR IS 
CONFIRMED AND THE CHAIN IS LONGER THAN I WROTE:** `machine_cats.c` adds footbridge -> `bus_pci_add(..., 
\"ali_m1543\")` -> `PCIINIT(ali_m1543)` for MACHINE_CATS calls `bus_isa_init(..., 0x7c000000, 0x80000000)` with NO 
`BUS_ISA_EXTERNAL_PIC`, putting the 8259 at **0x7c000020 / 0x7c0000a0** -- the same mapping the `isaorder` row already 
measured.  NetWinder gets the same ports via `symphony_83c553`.  A command-port write that is not ICW1 / 0x0a / 0x0b / 
0x0c / an EOI form / the two non-halting TODOs (0x68, 0xc0-0xc7) falls to `:140`, and **`idata = 0` is enough -- a 
legal OCW2 NOP on real hardware.**  RANKED #2 of the guest-reachable halts.",
