     "title": "dev_lunafb.c halts the CPU from four sites on ordinary framebuffer offsets, on a machine this project 
actually boots -- and the file had never been queued at all",
>    "state_note": "`dev_lunafb.c` halts the CPU on ordinary framebuffer offsets: the `lunafb_a` and `lunafb_b` 
`default:` arms and a ROP-not-all-ones write all do `cpu->running = 0`.  Sites verified by grep at HEAD: `:93`, 
`:160`, `:166`, `:185`. *** THIS FILE HAD NEVER BEEN QUEUED -- `lunafb` had ZERO mentions in the entire ledger before 
this row. ***  It is installed unconditionally from `DEVINIT(luna88k)`, and luna88k is a machine this project HAS a 
rig for and DOES boot, which is what separates it from the ip30 case (advertised but no in-tree boot image). 
REACHABILITY **BELIEVED, NOT CONFIRMED**: the seat's claim is that `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) implementing only 
`BMAP_RFCNT` and `BMAP_BMSEL`, so every other offset in that window stops the CPU, and that a comment naming 
`0xb1000000` for BOTH is wrong while the `#define`s are right.  It also claims OpenBSD's `omfb_clear_framebuffer` uses 
BMSEL, which IS implemented, so the boot path is BELIEVED clear of the halt.  **None of that is re-verified here.**  
FAULT (member 5) is architecturally available on M88K and `return 0` would deliver it -- today the halt runs first. 
*** PROVENANCE, AND IT IS A RECORDS FAILURE OF MINE: this was named by the 2026-09-17 `exithunt' seat, which returned 
FOUR findings.  I filed TWO (`ip30abortkill`, `mvme187memcoob`), acted on the abort() one -- which produced a measured 
rung-3 witness and a real HIGH -- and LEFT THESE TWO UNQUEUED.  `lunafb` had ZERO mentions anywhere in the ledger, so 
that file had never been queued at all.  **That is the UNREAD-REVIEW class, which this project has three times found 
real defects sitting in**, and here the review was read and then half-acted-on, which is the same shape one step 
later. *** *** CORRECTED 2026-09-17: NOT UNCONDITIONAL, AND NOT ON THE DEFAULT LUNA BOOT.  MY ROW'S FRAMING WAS THE 
WRONG WAY ROUND. ***  I filed it as being on 'a machine this project ACTUALLY BOOTS', implying boot reachability.  
**`dev_luna88k.c:1098-1101` adds lunafb ONLY `if (devinit->machine->x11_md.in_use)`** -- so **default `-E luna88k`, 
the serial `login:` rig, does NOT install it at all**, and the hunt's 'CONFIRMED probe on unmodified -E luna-88k' is 
FALSE. The rest of the reading HOLDS and is sharper than I filed it: `lunafb_a` is 512 KB (`0x80000`) at 0xB1000000 
implementing only `BMAP_RFCNT` (offset 0) and `BMAP_BMSEL` (offset 0x40000), so everything else in it stops the CPU; 
**the comment naming `0xb1000000` for BOTH arms is wrong while the `#define`s are right**; and `lunafb_b` is far 
LARGER than I stressed -- 0xB1100000..0xE1000000, roughly **767 MB**, implementing only plane-1 detect, 16 bytes of 
PALLET2, and `BMAP_FN + 4*ROP_THROUGH` **only when the write is 0xffffffff**. **OpenBSD's `omfb_clear_framebuffer` is 
CLEAR of the halt**, traced step by step (RFCNT write, PLANEMASK 0xff, `ROPFUNC[5] = ~0` which is exactly the one 
implemented all-ones slot, bitmap zeros, PLANEMASK 0x01).  What DOES halt under `-X` is later console drawing writing 
`ROPFUNC[5] = lmask` with `lmask != 0xffffffff` -- putchar, not the clear path. **DEMOTED to LOW-MED and RANKED #4**: 
real, but X11-only.  FAULT is the available member on M88K and is not what ships, because `#220`'s running-latch 
returns MEMORY_ACCESS_FAILED first.",
