     "state": "closed",
>    "witness": "none -- documented-only.  ANSWERED BY READING WITH A DENOMINATOR on both sides: armmsr's reachability 
by mode enumeration (7 handled, 25 unhandled, none producible by a correct in-tree kernel), and ip30's by 
gate_asan_sweep.sh:593 running ip30 green.  The ruling is ip30abortkill first.",
     "commits": [],
     "opened": "2026-09-17",
     "title": "the order between the two open HIGHs rests on real-guest reachability measured for neither -- ip30's 
aborts are on no in-tree path, and armmsr's probe is built but unrun",
>    "state_note": "*** BOTH OPEN HIGHs' SEVERITIES REST ON REAL-GUEST REACHABILITY THAT IS MEASURED FOR NEITHER, AND 
THE ORDER BETWEEN THEM WAS SET WITHOUT IT.  Named by the Fable adjudicator 2026-09-17 as the question the adjudication 
was not being asked. ***  RUNG IS A FLOOR ON ADMISSIBILITY; SEVERITY ORDERS THE QUEUE -- that is settled carrier 
doctrine.  What is not settled is the severity input.  **`ip30abortkill`:** rung-3 probe witness, measured SIGABRT.  
But `gate_asan_sweep.sh:593` runs `for s in ip12 ip28 ip30 ip32 ip35` and is GREEN, so **the ISR aborts are on no path 
anything in-tree travels**; only one gate names ip30 at all; there is no in-tree Octane boot image; and the device 
header says it is 'just comprised of hardcoded guesses so far.'  The defect is real for a guest that touches those 
addresses -- which a booting Linux/IP30 would, since the code cites that very driver -- and hypothetical for anything 
running here today.  **`armmsrmodekill`:** kills the PROCESS (not a halt, not a probe-only path) on ARM, whose decode 
IS exercised across testarm/barearm/cats.  Its real-guest reachability is ALSO unmeasured -- and 
`regress/arm_msrmode_probe.py` is BUILT and unrun, so that measurement is one run away.  **DISPOSITION: do not re-rank 
on argument.  Run the arm probe; it is the cheap measurement that informs the order of the expensive work.**  This row 
exists so the ranking is not silently decided by which defect was found most recently.  *** THE RANKING IS SETTLED BY 
READING WITH A DENOMINATOR: `ip30abortkill` FIRST. ***  And the distinction that settles it is worth more than the 
verdict: **the gap on ip30 is 'we do not run that OS here'; the gap on armmsr is 'that OS would not do this.'  Only 
the second kind of gap demotes a defect.**  A process death on the documented interrupt path of the guest the machine 
exists to run outranks a process death that requires the guest to program a reserved mode. **Q1 -- CAN A REAL ARM 
GUEST REACH `armmsrmodekill`?  NO.**  `arm_save_register_bank` / `arm_load_register_bank` switch on `cpsr & 0x1f`: **7 
arms handled, 25 of 32 unhandled.**  Handled: USR32 0x10, FIQ32 0x11, IRQ32 0x12, SVC32 0x13, ABT32 0x17, UND32 0x1b, 
SYS32 0x1f. The unhandled 25 break down as: **26-bit modes 0x00-0x03** (`cpu_arm.h` names them and `grep -c 'case 
ARM_MODE_USR26'` across src/ is ZERO; CATS/testarm/barearm are SA-110/SA1110 and the in-tree NetBSD `armreg.h` does 
not even define 26-bit modes); **0x04-0x0F unused**; **0x14/0x15/0x18/0x19/0x1c/0x1d/0x1e reserved** -- DDI 0100I 
A2.5.7 says *'If any other value is programmed into the mode bits M[4:0], the result is UNPREDICTABLE'*, and there are 
NO MSR exceptions; **0x16 Monitor** (TrustZone, not on any in-tree CPU); **0x1a Hyp** (ARMv7-A virt, likewise). A real 
kernel writes the named constants plus I/F -- `msr cpsr_c, #PSR_SVC32_MODE|I32_bit|F32_bit` (0xD3), IRQ 0x12, USR on 
process start, SYS for some copy paths -- and locore is `bic r0,r0,#0x1f ; orr r0,r0,#<legal> ; msr cpsr_c,r0`.  
**Userspace cannot do it at all**: `X(msr_imm)` strips the c-byte in USR32 (`mask &= 0xff000000`). **Q2 -- THE SECOND 
PATH, which I had not considered and which is the one that could have been worse:** `MSR SPSR` is UNVALIDATED, and 
`MOVS PC, LR` / `LDM {..,pc}^` copies SPSR into CPSR **with no mode check**, after which Load dies identically.  But a 
correct kernel's SPSR is either the CPSR saved at exception entry (a legal mode) or a sigreturn frame, and Linux's 
`valid_user_regs` rejects anything whose MODE_MASK is not USR/USR26 and forces USR32.  **So the path exists and no 
correct kernel drives it.** **CONSEQUENCE FOR `armmsrmodekill`:** it stays a real process death and a real fix -- a 
hostile or buggy guest reaches it, and 25 of 32 encodings are unhandled -- but its SEVERITY is now measured rather 
than assumed, and it does NOT outrank ip30.  **`highrankunmeasured` is answered on the armmsr side by READING; the 
ip30 side stands as measured (gate_asan_sweep runs ip30 green, so the aborts are on no in-tree path).**",
