    {
     "id": "ip22legacyabort",
     "commits": [],
     "opened": "2026-09-17",
     "title": "dev_sgi_ip22.c carries TWO more guest-reachable fatal()+abort() pairs in the write arms of 
local0/local1 irq mask -- the same message family as #531's four, on an advertised machine",
>    "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-17 by the #531 pass-2 opus5 seat as the nearest SIBLING of ip30abortkill.  Two 
structurally identical `fatal(\"TODO: ip22 legacy interrupt rewrite!\"); abort();' pairs in dev_sgi_ip22.c, in the 
WRITE arms of the local0 and local1 interrupt masks.  *** THE CENSUS LESSON RECURS: these carry no `exit(', so the 
same three exit( sweeps that walked past #531's four sites walk past these too.  A census keyed on `exit(' cannot see 
a host death spelled abort(). ***  Ranked HIGH on the same warrant as #531: guest-reachable host death on an 
advertised machine. The disposition is likely the same as #531's -- heart.h has no ip22 equivalent, so the option-set 
work starts from the file's own sibling handlers.  LINE NUMBERS VERIFIED 2026-09-17 by opening the file: :314 and :338 
exactly, 2 abort() and 0 exit( in dev_sgi_ip22.c.  Grade upgraded BELIEVED -> CONFIRMED.  *** SEVERITY CORRECTED HIGH 
-> LOW THE SAME DAY IT WAS FILED, AND THE CORRECTION IS MINE TO OWN: I RANKED IT AS A SIBLING OF #531 WITHOUT CHECKING 
THE ONE FACT THAT MADE #531 LIVE. ***  #531's warrant is that `-E sgi -e ip30' is an ADVERTISED machine AND the device 
is actually installed -- its rung-3 probe reached the code and killed the host, empirically.  dev_sgi_ip22 is 
DIFFERENT and the difference is decisive:  (a) `dev_sgi_ip22_init' has exactly TWO call sites in the whole tree, 
machine_sgi.c:270 and :278, and BOTH ARE COMMENTED OUT;  (b) the file defines DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_ip22) but NO 
DEVINIT(sgi_ip22), so the autodev / device_add route cannot register it either;  (c) it still COMPILES IN 
(Makefile.skel lists dev_sgi_ip22.o), which is why an abort()-census finds it and a reachability question does not get 
asked.  *** THE DEVICE IS NEVER IN ANY MACHINE'S MEMORY MAP, SO NO GUEST CAN REACH EITHER abort(). ***  CONSEQUENCE 
FOR THE WITNESS LADDER, and it is why this cannot simply be 'done later': NO WITNESS EXISTS OR CAN EXIST AT RUNGS 2-4 
while the device is uninstalled, and installing it to reach the site is exactly what the ladder names as LAUNDERING -- 
'a machine or device_add introduced IN ORDER TO reach the site is laundering, not witnessing'.  So this is 
DOCUMENTED-ONLY unless and until something installs the device for its own reasons.  IT REMAINS WORTH KEEPING rather 
than dropping: the sites are dead code carrying a host kill, and the day anyone re-enables those two commented lines 
they become live immediately, with no review in between.  *** THE CENSUS LESSON STANDS UNCHANGED AND IS THE REAL VALUE 
HERE: 2 abort() and 0 exit( in this file, so the same three exit( sweeps that walked past #531's four sites walk past 
these two. ***  A census keyed on `exit(' cannot see a host death spelled abort() -- whether or not the code is 
reachable.",
     "entries": [
      {
       "seat": "opus5",
       "phase": "review",
       "date": "2026-09-17",
       "grade": "CONFIRMED",
     "commits": [],
     "opened": "2026-09-18",
     "title": "a row prescribed BY NAME in a seat's fix-design answer can be lost even when #490's 
diff-against-the-PRETABLE rule is obeyed, because the prescription never entered the PRETABLE -- and a PRETABLE that 
mis-classifies the cell CERTIFIES the omission",
>    "state_note": "*** THE FOURTH RECORDED INSTANCE OF THE UNREAD-REVIEW CLASS, AND THE MOST PRECISE: THE MISSING ROW 
WAS PRESCRIBED BY NAME, IN ONE LINE, IN A DURABLE ARTIFACT, BEFORE THE FIX SHIPPED. ***  
regress/artifacts/ip30abortkill/FIXDIFF_grok_20260917.txt:239 reads: '5. `isr 20 rd' / `isr 28 rd' -- load does not 
clobber ISR; load's VALUE is not the seeded ISR (don't-mirror)'.  BOTH rows, explicitly.  The round shipped `isr 20 rd 
val' and not its sibling, and it then cost a full extra kill table and a nine-seat adjudication pass to rediscover -- 
with agy REQUIRING it and grok having to point at its own earlier answer.  The opus5 seat independently reported the 
same pattern: grok's design pass prescribed six closing rows and items 1, 2, 4 and 5 did not ship.  *** THE STRUCTURAL 
GAP, WHICH IS THE PART WORTH KEEPING: THE CARRIER'S EXISTING RULE CANNOT CATCH THIS. ***  After #490 the rule is 'a 
row list rewritten from a panel's findings is DIFFED against the pre-table block's own prescribed rows, not merely 
extended with the new ones.'  That binds the PRETABLE.  Here the prescription NEVER ENTERED A PRETABLE -- it lived in 
a SEAT'S FIX-DESIGN ANSWER, and nothing diffs a row list against that.  So the #490 rule was obeyed and the row was 
still lost: the diff ran against the wrong source document.  AND THE PRETABLE MADE IT WORSE RATHER THAN NEUTRAL: 
PRETABLE.out listed BOTH read arms as 'decline, no value to pin', so the one artifact #490 DOES mandate diffing 
against positively asserted that neither row was owed.  *A pre-table block that mis-classifies a cell does not merely 
fail to catch the omission; it CERTIFIES it.*  grok's correction is one sentence: A DECLINE HAS A VALUE, AND IT IS 0.  
PROPOSED REMEDY, FILED NOT BUILT (a harness change owing two negative controls): a round whose fix design was reviewed 
by a seat DIFFS ITS FINAL ROW LIST AGAINST THAT SEAT'S PRESCRIBED ROWS, as #490 mandates for the PRETABLE -- and the 
PRETABLE should CARRY those prescriptions forward so the two documents cannot disagree silently.  Cheapest mechanical 
form is existence-only: if a FIXDIFF_<seat>_*.txt sits beside the killtable dir, each of its numbered prescriptions 
must map to a shipped row or an explicit no-row sentence.  *** THE LAST FIVE UNREAD ANSWERS READ 2026-09-18 
(panel_20260827_134019).  23 OF 23 NOW READ -- the section-G backlog is CLEARED. ***  This was a pass-1 DESIGN panel 
on a now-CLOSED row, so it was read for the question `seatprescriptiondiff' exists to ask: DID THE PRESCRIPTIONS 
SURVIVE INTO THE SHIPPED WORK?  *** AND THE ANSWER IS YES -- A NEGATIVE RESULT THAT BOUNDS THE ROW I FILED THIS 
MORNING. ***  agy prescribed three items.  (1) the predicate fix -- SHIPPED, dev_wdc.c now triggers on WDCTL_RST 
(0x04) and not WDCTL_4BIT (0x08), with the rising-edge `prev_ctl' machinery and a recorded brace hazard beside it.  
