re, bit 50 was never set, no tick announced, and four tick rows reddened FOR A REASON UNRELATED TO THE CELL UNDER TEST.  *** HAD I NOT CHECKED **WHICH** ROWS DIED I WOULD HAVE RECORDED THE WIDTH AXIS AS COVERED WHEN IT WAS NEVER TESTED. ***  A mutant that dies for the wrong reason misleads exactly as much as one that will not die; the carrier\u0027s three explanations apply and this is case 1, MIS-BUILT.  REPAIRED: anchored on guard #3 (:202, case 0x10020), verified by a dry-run diff that shows it landing in the right arm.  *** AND THE MIS-BUILD LEFT A REAL FINDING, KEPT RATHER THAN DISCARDED: THE DETECTOR DOES CATCH A WIDTH NARROWING AT imask0, WITH A FOUR-ROW SIGNATURE -- coverage on an arm this round never enumerated.  The tick rows collectively pin that imask0 accepts a 64-bit write.  Not a floor cell (the fix does not touch imask0), but real, and found by accident. ***  V7 ESCAPING EXACTLY AS PREDICTED IS ITSELF A RESULT: a written prediction of an ESCAPE landing is worth as much as a kill landing, because it says the disclosure is HONEST rather than a hedge.  SIGNATURES DISAMBIGUATE: V1 reds `xbit rev\u0027 ALONE; V6 reds `latch tick\u0027 AND `xbit rev\u0027.  So `xbit rev\u0027 is not a pure collision detector and only `latch tick\u0027 separates the two causes -- the rows are COUPLED, the same shape already recorded for xbit isr / kill 20 wr txt, and a future reader pruning `redundant\u0027 rows would destroy the discrimination.  TABLE 5 IS RUNNING to re-verify V2 and V8 and to regression-check V1 (PRETABLE5.out 04:30:15 against 04:30:25)."
