a reassert function), NOT this de-fatalisation. **MEMBER 5 FAULT is AVAILABLE and WRONG.**  `memory_rw.c` turns a device `return 0` into `EXCEPTION_DBE` on MIPS, so it works -- but these offsets are NOT unmapped space: they have storage, comments, and a PROM that already talks to the neighbour at 0x10018.  `dev_unreadable.c` is the FAULT idiom and is for holes.  *Note the brief's own framing was weak here and the seat said so: `abort()` firing before any `return 0` is a description of the current kill, not a design warrant either way.* **Q3 -- THE writeflag TEST IS A SEPARATE DEFECT AND IS NOT OPTIONAL.**  This is the `asicindexcase` / `mvme187memcoob` shape: the kill is currently also the direction check.  De-fatalise 0x10020 without adding the test and **the host lives on with a guest-clobbered ISR** -- worse than today.  0x10028 shares the missing test and does not corrupt today (`&= ~0` is a no-op) but gets it anyway, so the twins stay twins.  0x10030 already has it and its reads work NOW -- an existing accept-side channel that must not break.  Owed row: a load at 0x10020 leaves ISR unchanged (seed via a write, `lw`, read back at 0x10030). **Q4 -- YOU DO NOT DECLINE A TICK; YOU STUB THE CALLBACK.**  Members 1-6 presume an access with a value to refuse, and the `:78` abort has none -- the guest already completed a LEGAL write to an IMPLEMENTED register.  No new option-set member is minted: this is member 3 applied to a callback, exactly as `#516` did for `lca_interrupt_assert`.  The tick also does not implement its own comment (no compare of 0x20000 against 0x30000), so a member-7 timer would have to ADD that, not merely delete the abort. **Q5 -- DE-ESCALATION ACCEPT ROWS.**  `0x10000` is not 'maybe an accept row' -- for the tick it IS the accept row, because that write is what used to arm the host death; it owes a companion row that the host SURVIVES the next tick.  Neighbours to pin: 0x20, 0x10000, 0x10018, 0x10030 READ, 0x20000, 0x30000.  **The latch must be TWO BITS, not one** (tick vs ISR-recalc): a shared bit lets the first shape silence the second, which is LCA's assert/deassert lesson. **Q6 -- SIBLINGS, GREPPED.**  `dev_sgi_ip19.c` and `dev_sgi_ip20.c` use `debug()` + `return 1` for unknown offsets; **this file's OWN `default:` does the same**.  So the ISR arms are the file's outliers, not the house idiom -- the same upstream-residue finding that settled `sgireunmapped`. **WITNESS LIMIT, STATED:** the probe covers the three ACCESS kills and does NOT cover the TICK kill.  `tickshift` is 16, so the tick fires every 65536 cycles and a handful of `step`s cannot reach it -- that needs `continue`/free-run, which is the same step-vs-free-run distinction the carrier already records for host-timer paths.  The tick row is OWED and is not claimed.  ## *** PANEL PASS 1 -- FOUR CORRECTIONS, TWO OF THEM TO WRONG RECORDS I WROTE THIS SESSION. *** (7 of 8 scriptable transports; codex still running at write time; Fable held for last.) **(A) DISPOSITION 1 IS WRONG AS RECORDED, and filing the follow-up row on it would ship a false premise.**  I recorded *'Member 7 IMPLEMENT is NOT available.'*  **The correct statement is AVAILABLE-BUT-UNWARRANTED.**  What does not exist is `cpu_interrupt()`; the LIVE API -- `interrupt_assert`/`interrupt_deassert` through `struct interrupt` -- exists and is exactly what `dev_sgi_ip32.c` uses for CRIME.  So member 7 is not blocked by the absence of an API; it is declined because DELIVERY IS A SUBSYSTEM nobody has costed (no INTERRUPT_CONNECT for ip30, the 16550 already on `cpu.2`, and a deleted `8 + x` numbering).  *The distinction is not pedantic: 'not available' would make the filed follow-up row unbuildable by definition, when in fact it is ordinary unscheduled work.*  (glm, who noticed that my own finding names the live API two sentences after declaring the member unavailable.) **(B) DISPOSITION 4'S WORD 'SEPARATE' IS THE WEAKEST THING IN THE PACKET.**  grok and the kimi substitute reached this independently.  The missing `writeflag` test is **the store-arm of THIS de-fatalisation** -- the same three `case` labels, the same function, the same common tail -- so it is ONE DIFF either way: *if you keep a store, a load must not execute it; if you decline the store, the load is already safe.*  **Calling it 'separate' is what made my own Q4 (scope-creep?) askable at all**, so the framing manufactured the question.  It is genuinely separate only at `0x10030`, which is already writeflag-tested.  kimi's sharper form: at 0x10020/0x10028 the missing test **is the read-kill's entry condition**, not a second defect. **(C) MY Q2 WAS REFUTED BY MY OWN PACKET** -- independently by kimi and deepseek.  I asked whether keeping `d->isr = idata` is member 4 given that the store updates *'a register nothing reads for any purpose'*.  **The same packet states that `case 0x10030`'s READ returns `d->isr`.**  The register IS read, by a path the packet itself documents and the witness measures (`acc 30 rd` alive).  The question was malformed; the answer is that the store feeds a live read path, which is member 4 ACCEPT on its own terms and not member 6. **(D) TWO WARRANTS SHARPENED RATHER THAN OVERTURNED.**  Disposition 3's warrant cited a PROM that 'already talks to 0x10018' -- but 0x10018 is a NEIGHBOUR, so that sentence is surplus to the claim it was supporting (grok).  Disposition 6 holds, and its in-tree warrant is better taken from CRIME's *'one bit per site, never shared'* than from LCA's two-functions case; the hazard is concrete -- **one shared bit lets a Set-ISR write silence the timer-tick diagnostic forever** (grok, minimax, agy).  Disposition 7 holds AS A CENSUS but does NOT pick member 2 over member 3: the `fatal()` already exists, so de-fatalising KEEPS it and latches (grok). **STILL OPEN AND NOT INVENTED:** minimax says Q3 -- what a read at 0x10020 should RETURN post-fix -- **cannot be settled from the packet** and needs an IP30/HEART source, and there is no HEART manual 

